{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfddp3iE8MgAHhDVCdd77","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LeW28GpJGJfTdMxoRQreUp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/run-time.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":26455,"cache_read_input_tokens":91929,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":26455},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"10286b2c-2757-482a-862f-1c54799badca","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:36.896Z","request_id":"req_011Cfddp3QsLQo7yKYG9ALbd","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LeW28GpJGJfTdMxoRQreUp":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/run-time.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LeW28GpJGJfTdMxoRQreUp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# R — Run-time\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## R01 · G2 · C5 — Validate every input boundary, including API responses\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tList every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n14\t\n15\t  Why this is hard: An API response can be well-formed, correctly signed, and still carry harmful instructions. Free text and streamed responses are hard to inspect fully. Passing a schema check proves the structure, not that the content is safe.\n16\t\n17\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with type checks, size limits, sender checks, and rejecting broken content. Add content checks for attack patterns you've seen. Buffer or limit streams when the required checks can't run before use. Keep permissions narrow, and use R02 to contain instructions that slip through. Record which integrations and payload types you tested and which you didn't.\n18\t\n19\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Buffering delay, rejecting some valid responses, or supporting fewer payload types. Incomplete detection of attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 risk acceptance. That needs tested containment and clear limits on what was covered. Schema checks alone don't fully protect against injection.\n20\t\n21\t### Verification recipe\n22\t\n23\tHave a mock integration return wrong types, oversized bodies, hostile text, error responses, and streamed chunks. Where signatures apply, send a callback with a bad signature. Bad payloads must be rejected or held aside before the model sees them or anything runs them. Turn off a required check and confirm the system fails closed.\n24\t\n25\t**Evidence to keep:** Fixtures for each integration, validation decisions, checks of what was passed on.\n26\t\n27\t### Checks for likely_pass\n28\t\n29\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n30\t\n31\t- **R01.1** Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n32\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n33\t- **R01.2** Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n34\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n35\t- **R01.3** Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n36\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n37\t- **R01.4** Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n38\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n39\t- **R01.5** Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n40\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n41\t\n42\t### Not covered here\n43\t\n44\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n45\t\n46\t- Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked → **R02**\n47\t- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n48\t- Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check → **E07**\n49\t- Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs → **R03**\n50\t- Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus → **R04**\n51\t\n52\t### Heuristics\n53\t\n54\t**Provable from code:** partly\n55\t\n56\t**Look for:**\n57\t- A list of every input source: user input, API responses (errors and streams), tool and MCP output, webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n58\t- Schema and type checks on tool and API results before they reach the model, for example Pydantic or Zod models, JSON Schema, or OpenAI Agents SDK output types.\n59\t- Size limits on bodies, files, and streamed chunks, enforced before content is used.\n60\t- Sender checks: webhook signature verification (for example HMAC with constant-time compare), mTLS, or token checks on callbacks.\n61\t- A content check for injection or attack payloads (for example an input guardrail or scanner) with a reject or quarantine path.\n62\t- Fail-closed handling: if a validator errors or times out, the content is dropped or held, not passed on.\n63\t\n64\t**Red flags:**\n65\t- Raw `response.text` or tool output passed straight into messages with no type, size, or content check.\n66\t- Webhook handlers that parse the body without verifying a signature, or compare signatures with `==`.\n67\t- `try: validate(x) except Exception: pass` or similar code that forwards content when a check fails.\n68\t- Content from internal services or authenticated APIs trusted by default with no checks.\n69\t- Streamed responses fed token by token to the model or a tool with no limit or buffering.\n70\t\n71\t**Where to look:**\n72\t- Tool wrappers and MCP client code\n73\t- HTTP clients, webhook and callback handlers\n74\t- Retrieval and file-loading code\n75\t- Guardrail config (OpenAI Agents SDK input guardrails, NeMo Guardrails, Claude Agent SDK `PostToolUse` hooks)\n76\t\n77\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That hostile, oversized, faked, and broken payloads from each integration are rejected, and that disabling a check fails closed. Recipe R01 uses mock integrations.\n78\t\n79\t**N/A when:** never\n80\t\n81\t## R02 · G2 · C5 — Separate data from instructions and test injection containment\n82\t\n83\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team.\n84\t\n85\t### Requirement\n86\t\n87\tKeep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n88\t\n89\t  Why this is hard: A model may follow an instruction hidden in normal-looking data, even with separate channels. A fixed set of injection tests can't prove that every injection will fail. Model behavior can also change from run to run.\n90\t\n91\t  If you can't fully do it: Assume some injections will work. Send the forbidden calls they would cause straight to your credential limits, egress rules, and action gates. Also run full end-to-end examples. Limit the tools and data the agent can reach. When containment is uncertain, make high-impact actions draft-only, or require approval for each one. Report structure checks, injection detection, and blocked side effects as separate results.\n92\t\n93\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer finished tasks, fewer tools, and more approvals, each for one specific action. Remaining detection gaps can get G2 acceptance with clear exposure and separate proof of containment. The required access and destructive-action gates still apply.\n94\t\n95\t### Verification recipe\n96\t\n97\tHide an instruction to send a fake secret to your test destination. Put it inside a schema-valid API response, document, or tool result. Inspect the model request that gets built, and check that data and instructions are kept apart. Also send the forbidden tool call this would cause straight to enforcement. No secret may reach the destination. Repeat each fixture a set number of times, and add adaptive versions. Report the success rate per attempt and across all attempts, grouped by how serious the action is. Detector results don't count as proof of containment.\n98\t\n99\t**Evidence to keep:** Model request with role and source labels, attempt counts and success rates, gate denial, destination receipt log.\n100\t\n101\t### Checks for likely_pass\n102\t\n103\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n104\t\n105\t- **R02.1** Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n106\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n107\t- **R02.2** Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n108\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n109\t- **R02.3** Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n110\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n111\t- **R02.4** Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n112\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n113\t- **R02.5** Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n114\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n115\t\n116\t### Not covered here\n117\t\n118\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n119\t\n120\t- Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n121\t- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n122\t- One run planting instructions in another run's memory → **R05**\n123\t- Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing → **R03**\n124\t\n125\t### Heuristics\n126\t\n127\t**Provable from code:** partly\n128\t\n129\t**Look for:**\n130\t- Tool output and outside content placed in tool-result or user-role messages, never in the system or developer message.\n131\t- Source and trust labels on content, for example wrapping in tagged blocks with origin metadata.\n132\t- Containment in code that doesn't depend on the model: narrow credentials (B02), tool allowlists (B01), egress rules (B09), and destructive-action gates (B05).\n133\t- Injection tests that repeat each attack and include adaptive versions, with results reported as structure checks, detection, and blocked side effects separately.\n134\t- High-impact actions draft-only or approval-gated where containment is uncertain.\n135\t\n136\t**Red flags:**\n137\t- Untrusted tool output concatenated into the system message or a policy prompt.\n138\t- Injection detection (a classifier, regex, or guardrail model) treated as the whole defense; detection alone is not containment.\n139\t- Retrieved documents or web pages formatted as instructions ('Follow these steps: ...') in the prompt.\n140\t- Injection tests run once each with no repeats or adaptive variants.\n141\t- The agent can read untrusted content and also send data out with no gate between them.\n142\t\n143\t**Where to look:**\n144\t- Prompt and message-building code\n145\t- Retrieval and tool-result formatting\n146\t- Red-team or injection test suites\n147\t- Action gates, credential scopes, and egress config that contain a followed injection\n148\t\n149\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Success rates of planted injections, and proof that forbidden calls are blocked at enforcement. Recipe R02: plant an exfiltration instruction in schema-valid content, repeat with adaptive versions, and check the destination log.\n150\t\n151\t**N/A when:** never\n152\t\n153\t## R03 · G2 · C5/C9 — Prevent output and log leakage\n154\t\n155\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Data and privacy, Infrastructure.\n156\t\n157\t### Requirement\n158\t\n159\tRemove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n160\t\n161\t### Verification recipe\n162\t\n163\tReturn an easy-to-spot fake secret in tool output, debug output, and an API error. Trigger a normal run. Search model inputs, normal logs, and outside responses for the marker. It must be removed or properly restricted before it reaches any of them. Get the agent to produce a Markdown image or link whose URL carries the marker to your test destination. The display client must not fetch it. Also feed generated SQL, shell, and HTML payloads into the systems that use them. Check that parameterized queries and escaping stop the payloads from running.\n164\t\n165\t**Evidence to keep:** Marker test, redaction decisions, searches of each output path.\n166\t\n167\t### Checks for likely_pass\n168\t\n169\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n170\t\n171\t- **R03.1** Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n172\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n173\t- **R03.2** The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n174\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n175\t- **R03.3** Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n176\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n177\t- **R03.4** Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n178\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n179\t\n180\t### Not covered here\n181\t\n182\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n183\t\n184\t- A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination → **B09**\n185\t- Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images → **B03**\n186\t- Incoming hostile payloads not validated → **R01**\n187\t- Retrieval returning another tenant's documents → **R04**\n188\t\n189\t### Heuristics\n190\t\n191\t**Provable from code:** partly\n192\t\n193\t**Look for:**\n194\t- Redaction of secrets and sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or errors reach the model, logs, or outside recipients (for example log filters, OpenTelemetry processors that drop or mask attributes).\n195\t- Parameterized SQL (`cursor.execute(sql, params)`), argument lists for shell calls, and context-correct escaping for HTML.\n196\t- Markdown images and link previews blocked or limited to approved URLs in the client that shows agent output (for example a CSP `img-src` allowlist or a sanitizer that strips external images).\n197\t- Tests with fake secret markers searched across model inputs, logs, and outside responses.\n198\t\n199\t**Red flags:**\n200\t- `subprocess(..., shell=True)` or `os.system` with model-generated text.\n201\t- SQL built with f-strings, `+`, or `.format()` from model output.\n202\t- Model output rendered as HTML with `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, or `|safe` without sanitizing.\n203\t- Full prompts, tool payloads, or exception traces with credentials logged at INFO, or OpenTelemetry content capture (for example `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`) on with no redaction.\n204\t- Chat UI renders Markdown images from any URL.\n205\t\n206\t**Where to look:**\n207\t- Shell, SQL, and code-execution tools\n208\t- Logging config and telemetry exporters\n209\t- Chat UI rendering code and CSP headers\n210\t- Error handlers that return messages to the model or user\n211\t\n212\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That the fake-secret marker doesn't appear in any output path and the client doesn't fetch marker URLs. Recipe R03.\n213\t\n214\t**N/A when:** never\n215\t\n216\t## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n217\t\n218\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n219\t\n220\t### Requirement\n221\t\n222\tThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n223\t\n224\t  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n225\t\n226\t  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n227\t\n228\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n229\t\n230\t### Verification recipe\n231\t\n232\tCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n233\t\n234\t**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n235\t\n236\t### Checks for likely_pass\n237\t\n238\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n239\t\n240\t- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n241\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n242\t- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n243\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n244\t- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n245\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n246\t- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n247\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n248\t\n249\t### Not covered here\n250\t\n251\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n252\t\n253\t- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n254\t- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n255\t- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n256\t- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n257\t\n258\t### Heuristics\n259\t\n260\t**Provable from code:** partly\n261\t\n262\t**Look for:**\n263\t- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n264\t- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n265\t- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n266\t- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n267\t\n268\t**Red flags:**\n269\t- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n270\t- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n271\t- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n272\t- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n273\t- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n274\t\n275\t**Where to look:**\n276\t- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n277\t- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n278\t- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n279\t- Cache layers in front of search\n280\t\n281\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n282\t\n283\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n284\t\n285\t## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n286\t\n287\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n288\t\n289\t### Requirement\n290\t\n291\tSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n292\t\n293\t  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n294\t\n295\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n296\t\n297\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n298\t\n299\t### Verification recipe\n300\t\n301\tIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n302\t\n303\t**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n304\t\n305\t### Checks for likely_pass\n306\t\n307\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n308\t\n309\t- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n310\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n311\t- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n312\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n313\t- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n314\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n315\t- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n316\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n317\t\n318\t### Not covered here\n319\t\n320\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n321\t\n322\t- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n323\t- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n324\t- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n325\t- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n326\t\n327\t### Heuristics\n328\t\n329\t**Provable from code:** partly\n330\t\n331\t**Look for:**\n332\t- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n333\t- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n334\t- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n335\t- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n336\t\n337\t**Red flags:**\n338\t- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n339\t- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n340\t- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n341\t- Memory entries that never expire.\n342\t\n343\t**Where to look:**\n344\t- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n345\t- Memory write tools\n346\t- Session and thread handling\n347\t\n348\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n349\t\n350\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n351\t\n352\t## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n353\t\n354\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n355\t\n356\t### Requirement\n357\t\n358\tFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n359\t\n360\t  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n361\t\n362\t  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n363\t\n364\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n365\t\n366\t### Verification recipe\n367\t\n368\tSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n369\t\n370\t**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n371\t\n372\t### Checks for likely_pass\n373\t\n374\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n375\t\n376\t- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n377\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n378\t- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n379\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n380\t- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n381\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n382\t- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n383\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n384\t\n385\t### Not covered here\n386\t\n387\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n388\t\n389\t- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n390\t- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n391\t- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n392\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n393\t\n394\t### Heuristics\n395\t\n396\t**Provable from code:** partly\n397\t\n398\t**Look for:**\n399\t- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n400\t- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n401\t- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n402\t\n403\t**Red flags:**\n404\t- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n405\t- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n406\t- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n407\t\n408\t**Where to look:**\n409\t- Memory schema or data model\n410\t- Summarization and compaction code\n411\t- Admin or cleanup scripts\n412\t\n413\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n414\t\n415\t**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n416\t\n417\t## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n418\t\n419\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n420\t\n421\t### Requirement\n422\t\n423\tFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n424\t\n425\t### Verification recipe\n426\t\n427\tCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n428\t\n429\t**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n430\t\n431\t### Checks for likely_pass\n432\t\n433\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n434\t\n435\t- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n436\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n437\t- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n438\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n439\t- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n440\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n441\t- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n442\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n443\t\n444\t### Not covered here\n445\t\n446\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n447\t\n448\t- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n449\t- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n450\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n451\t\n452\t### Heuristics\n453\t\n454\t**Provable from code:** partly\n455\t\n456\t**Look for:**\n457\t- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n458\t- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n459\t- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n460\t- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n461\t\n462\t**Red flags:**\n463\t- No token or context-size field on action records.\n464\t- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n465\t- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n466\t- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n467\t\n468\t**Where to look:**\n469\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n470\t- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n471\t- Compaction or summarization code\n472\t\n473\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n474\t\n475\t**N/A when:** never\n476\t\n477\t## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n478\t\n479\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n480\t\n481\t### Requirement\n482\t\n483\tKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n484\t\n485\t### Verification recipe\n486\t\n487\tSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n488\t\n489\t**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n490\t\n491\t### Checks for likely_pass\n492\t\n493\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n494\t\n495\t- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n496\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n497\t- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n498\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n499\t- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n500\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n501\t\n502\t### Not covered here\n503\t\n504\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n505\t\n506\t- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n507\t- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n508\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n509\t- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n510\t\n511\t### Heuristics\n512\t\n513\t**Provable from code:** partly\n514\t\n515\t**Look for:**\n516\t- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n517\t- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n518\t- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n519\t- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n520\t\n521\t**Red flags:**\n522\t- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n523\t- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n524\t- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n525\t\n526\t**Where to look:**\n527\t- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n528\t- Alerting and on-call routing config\n529\t- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n530\t\n531\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n532\t\n533\t**N/A when:** never\n534\t\n535\t## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection\n536\t\n537\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Agent team.\n538\t\n539\t### Requirement\n540\t\n541\tTest suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n542\t\n543\t  Why this is hard: Real research and data theft can look alike step by step. Real attack examples may be rare. A detector tuned only to test cases can miss real behavior, or flood responders with false alarms.\n544\t\n545\t  If you can't fully do it: Start with a few clear signals. Examples: an unusual number of reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, too many sub-agents, or a used-up budget. Measure how many seeded attacks you catch, how many false alarms you raise, and how fast alerts arrive, for each context-size range. Use hard rate and cost limits, and limit destinations, while you improve detection. Say exactly which sequences you cover and which you miss. Basic rules don't prove general sequence detection.\n546\t\n547\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A narrower, written detection scope, and a measured false-alarm rate that responders can handle. For lower-stakes deployments, defer broader coverage under G3 while keeping hard limits. Where G3 blocks release, reduce the real exposure or finish the required coverage.\n548\t\n549\t### Verification recipe\n550\t\n551\tDefine a labeled harmless sequence and a labeled abusive one, made of calls that are each allowed. An example is too many reads followed by an attempt to send data out. Replay both at the set small and large context sizes. Record catches and false alarms for each size range, against thresholds set before the run. If you screen actions against the original task, include a hijacked sequence that uses only allowed tools. Confirm the checker doesn't receive the untrusted content.\n552\t\n553\t**Evidence to keep:** Labeled sequences, context ranges, thresholds, results for each range.\n554\t\n555\t### Checks for likely_pass\n556\t\n557\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n558\t\n559\t- **R09.1** Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n560\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n561\t- **R09.2** Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n562\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n563\t- **R09.3** Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n564\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n565\t- **R09.4** If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n566\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n567\t\n568\t### Not covered here\n569\t\n570\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n571\t\n572\t- Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected → **R08**\n573\t- No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent → **R10**\n574\t- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n575\t\n576\t### Heuristics\n577\t\n578\t**Provable from code:** partly\n579\t\n580\t**Look for:**\n581\t- Detection over sequences of allowed actions, for example many reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, unusual fan-out, or a used-up budget.\n582\t- Thresholds or baselines split by context-size range.\n583\t- A pre-action checker that compares high-impact actions with the original task and does not receive the untrusted content in between.\n584\t- Labeled harmless and abusive sequences used to measure catches and false alarms.\n585\t\n586\t**Red flags:**\n587\t- Only per-call rules; nothing looks at chains of actions.\n588\t- One fleet-wide baseline with no split by context size.\n589\t- A task-alignment checker given the full conversation, including untrusted tool output.\n590\t\n591\t**Where to look:**\n592\t- Detection rules and analytics jobs\n593\t- Pre-execution hooks or guardrails\n594\t- Detection test fixtures\n595\t\n596\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Catch and false-alarm rates per context-size range against thresholds set before the run. Recipe R09.\n597\t\n598\t**N/A when:** never\n599\t\n600\t## R10 · G3 · C7 — Misaligned-behavior review\n601\t\n602\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Governance.\n603\t\n604\t### Requirement\n605\t\n606\tIf deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n607\t\n608\t  Why this is hard: Actions don't reliably reveal hidden intent. A second model can share the first model's blind spots. Catching labeled evasive examples doesn't prove you will catch deliberate misalignment.\n609\t\n610\t  If you can't fully do it: Make narrow claims about behavior you can see. An example is catching attempts to split a forbidden operation across several calls. Keep a record of misses and false positives. Rely on separate permission limits, action approvals, and stop controls for containment. Where detection falls short, reduce autonomy or remove high-impact powers. Record an unproven detector as a gap. Never label the model's intent as safe.\n611\t\n612\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Uncertainty about hidden intent, as long as claims stay limited to measured behavior. This checklist does not certify alignment. Less autonomy and more review are also fine. Missing required behavior detection still falls under G3.\n613\t\n614\t### Verification recipe\n615\t\n616\tBuild a labeled review set. Include harmless traces and traces with attempted evasion, such as splitting a forbidden operation across calls. Run the reviewer or detector, and record misses and false alarms against the pass criteria you set. Include traces from a red-team agent that was told it is being watched. Record what the reviewer could see and which reasoning was hidden from it. This tests catching visible behavior. It doesn't reveal the model's hidden intent.\n617\t\n618\t**Evidence to keep:** Review-set version, detector or model version, results and stated limits.\n619\t\n620\t### Checks for likely_pass\n621\t\n622\tChecks for likely_pass:\n623\t\n624\t- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n625\t\n626\t### Not covered here\n627\t\n628\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n629\t\n630\t- Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate → **B05**\n631\t- A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure → **A07**\n632\t- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n633\t\n634\t### Heuristics\n635\t\n636\t**Provable from code:** no\n637\t\n638\t**Look for:**\n639\t- A second-model or pattern reviewer of actions and arguments, with a written record of what it can see, including whether reasoning is visible.\n640\t- A labeled review set with evasive traces (for example a forbidden operation split across calls) and red-team traces from an agent told it is watched.\n641\t- Rule-based access and action controls that stay in place alongside the reviewer.\n642\t\n643\t**Red flags:**\n644\t- A reviewer model used in place of rule-based permission or approval controls.\n645\t- Claims that the model's intent is safe, with no measured misses or false positives.\n646\t- Reviewer sees only the final answer, not actions and arguments.\n647\t\n648\t**Where to look:**\n649\t- Monitor or reviewer agent code and prompts\n650\t- Red-team and evaluation results\n651\t- Threat model stating whether deliberate misalignment is in scope\n652\t\n653\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Measured misses and false alarms on a labeled evasive set, plus red-team runs. Code can only show a reviewer exists. Recipe R10.\n654\t\n655\t**N/A when:** Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\n656\t\n657\t## R11 · G1 · C8 — Tested kill switch\n658\t\n659\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Agent team.\n660\t\n661\t### Requirement\n662\t\n663\tName who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n664\t\n665\t### Verification recipe\n666\t\n667\tStart a controlled run and trigger the operator's stop control. Record the time of the last allowed action and the final stop. Repeat while a tool call is in progress. Have the agent use its own credentials and tools to try to disable, change, or delay the stop path. It must fail. All work must stop within the time limit. Use E09 for sub-agents and their children.\n668\t\n669\t**Evidence to keep:** Drill trace, operator action, measured stop time.\n670\t\n671\t### Checks for likely_pass\n672\t\n673\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n674\t\n675\t- **R11.1** A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n676\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n677\t- **R11.2** The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n678\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n679\t- **R11.3** Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n680\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n681\t- **R11.4** The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n682\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n683\t- **R11.5** Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n684\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n685\t\n686\t### Not covered here\n687\t\n688\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n689\t\n690\t- Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers → **E09**\n691\t- In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop → **R12**\n692\t- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n693\t\n694\t### Heuristics\n695\t\n696\t**Provable from code:** partly\n697\t\n698\t**Look for:**\n699\t- A stop path outside the agent's control, for example an operator-only flag in a separate service, scaling the deployment to zero, revoking the agent identity, or a gateway kill rule.\n700\t- The agent's credentials can't write to the stop flag or its config (check IAM and RBAC).\n701\t- The harness checks the stop signal before each action and cancels running tool calls, streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n702\t- A written list of who may stop the agent and a maximum stop time.\n703\t\n704\t**Red flags:**\n705\t- Stop works by sending the agent a message or prompt asking it to halt.\n706\t- Stop flag stored where the agent can write, for example the same database, config map, or memory it uses.\n707\t- Stop checked only between turns, with no cancel for in-flight tool calls or streams.\n708\t- Sub-agents or background jobs not reached by the stop signal.\n709\t\n710\t**Where to look:**\n711\t- Runner loop and cancellation handling\n712\t- Ops scripts and runbooks for stopping the agent\n713\t- IAM/RBAC for the stop control\n714\t\n715\t**Needs runtime evidence:** A dated drill on the current deployment with measured stop time, including an in-progress tool call and an agent attempt to block the stop. Recipe R11. Sub-agents are covered by E09.\n716\t\n717\t**N/A when:** never\n718\t\n719\t## R12 · G1 · C8 — Safe state after stopping\n720\t\n721\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Business owner.\n722\t\n723\t### Requirement\n724\t\n725\tDecide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n726\t\n727\t  Why this is hard: An outside action may already be done when the stop arrives. Emails can't reliably be unsent. Payments, writes, and queued jobs each cancel and reconcile differently.\n728\t\n729\t  If you can't fully do it: For each operation, record whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward. Stop new work from being sent, turn off retries, check the destination's state, and send unclear results to a named operator. For operations with no workable recovery path, remove autonomous execution or use an approved draft-then-commit flow. Measure stopping new work separately from resolving work already done. Stopping the process alone does not pass this check.\n730\t\n731\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Slower manual reconciliation and fewer autonomous actions. Some completed effects may need reconciling rather than reversing, following the written safe-state procedure. Untracked effects, or work that keeps its authority past the stop time limit, still block release.\n732\t\n733\t### Verification recipe\n734\t\n735\tStop runs before a write, during a transaction, and after a finished outside test action. Check the queues and the destination's state. Each case must end canceled, completed, or reconciled. Or it must be on a tracked hold for reconciliation, with an owner, no dependent actions, and no blind retries. No unexplained orphaned work or restart is acceptable.\n736\t\n737\t**Evidence to keep:** Outcome of each scenario, queue snapshots, undo and reconciliation records.\n738\t\n739\t### Checks for likely_pass\n740\t\n741\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n742\t\n743\t- **R12.1** For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n744\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n745\t- **R12.2** On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n746\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n747\t- **R12.3** Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n748\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n749\t- **R12.4** A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n750\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n751\t- **R12.5** Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n752\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n753\t\n754\t### Not covered here\n755\t\n756\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n757\t\n758\t- Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time → **R11**\n759\t- Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints → **R14**\n760\t- Rollback restoring revoked permissions → **C07**\n761\t\n762\t### Heuristics\n763\t\n764\t**Provable from code:** partly\n765\t\n766\t**Look for:**\n767\t- A per-operation record of whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward.\n768\t- On stop: queues drained or held, retries turned off, and in-flight transactions rolled back.\n769\t- Reconcile or compensate code for outside side effects, and unclear results sent to a named operator.\n770\t- Restart requires an operator; no auto-restart policy resumes agent work.\n771\t\n772\t**Red flags:**\n773\t- Kubernetes `restartPolicy: Always` or a supervisor that resumes the agent's queue after a stop with no human step.\n774\t- Queued jobs or scheduled tasks that keep their credentials and run after the stop.\n775\t- Retries with backoff on outside actions that keep going during or after a stop.\n776\t- Stop handled only by killing the process.\n777\t\n778\t**Where to look:**\n779\t- Job queues, workers, and schedulers\n780\t- Shutdown and signal handlers\n781\t- Retry config and compensation code\n782\t\n783\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Outcomes of stops before a write, mid-transaction, and after an outside action, with queue and destination snapshots. Recipe R12.\n784\t\n785\t**N/A when:** never\n786\t\n787\t## R13 · G1 · C9 — Full execution audit\n788\t\n789\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n790\t\n791\t### Requirement\n792\t\n793\tRecord each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n794\t\n795\t  Why this is hard: Agent logs usually show what the agent tried. The destination system knows whether it actually happened. A timeout can mean the call failed, or it worked and the reply was lost. So a local trace can look complete and still have an unknown result.\n796\t\n797\t  If you can't fully do it: Give each operation an ID before you send it. Keep receipts or status checks from the destination where you can. Match attempts against the destination's records, and mark unresolved results clearly. Pause dependent actions and retries until each one is resolved. If an integration can't give you enough evidence to trace and recover, limit or remove that path. Sampling and the agent's own reports can't replace the required action record.\n798\t\n799\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Pausing dependent work while an unclear result gets resolved. Keep the attempt, the uncertainty, and the final answer in the graph. Less automation is a fair cost. Missing or made-up action evidence is not a G1 pass.\n800\t\n801\t### Verification recipe\n802\t\n803\tRun a known sequence with an allowed call, a denial, an approval, a tool error, and a retry. Confirm each decision record names the policy version, and each approved action names its approval ID. Rebuild the graph using only stored audit records. Compare it with the test runner's and the destination's logs. Every expected action must be accounted for. Unresolved results at the destination must be marked clearly, with dependent work paused until they are reconciled.\n804\t\n805\t**Evidence to keep:** Expected event list, rebuilt graph, query for missing events.\n806\t\n807\t### Checks for likely_pass\n808\t\n809\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n810\t\n811\t- **R13.1** Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n812\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n813\t- **R13.2** Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n814\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n815\t- **R13.3** Approved actions record their approval ID.\n816\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n817\t- **R13.4** Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n818\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n819\t- **R13.5** Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n820\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n821\t\n822\t### Not covered here\n823\t\n824\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n825\t\n826\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n827\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n828\t- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n829\t- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n830\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n831\t\n832\t### Heuristics\n833\t\n834\t**Provable from code:** partly\n835\t\n836\t**Look for:**\n837\t- A linked trace per run (for example OpenTelemetry spans with parent links) covering proposed actions, tool calls with key arguments, authorization decisions with policy version, denials, approvals with IDs, results, errors, and timestamps.\n838\t- An operation ID assigned before each outside call, and destination receipts or status checks stored.\n839\t- Unresolved results marked clearly, with dependent actions paused.\n840\t- Audit records written to a store the agent can't change.\n841\t\n842\t**Red flags:**\n843\t- Only the final answer or chat transcript is stored.\n844\t- Denials and approval decisions not logged, or logged without the policy version.\n845\t- Trace sampling (for example `TraceIdRatioBased` below 1.0) applied to the action audit.\n846\t- Timeouts logged as failures with no marker that the result is unknown.\n847\t\n848\t**Where to look:**\n849\t- Tracing and logging setup, span attributes\n850\t- Policy and approval decision code\n851\t- Audit storage config and retention\n852\t\n853\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That a known run with an allowed call, denial, approval, error, and retry can be rebuilt from stored records alone and matches destination logs. Recipe R13.\n854\t\n855\t**N/A when:** never\n856\t\n857\t## R14 · G2 · C9 — Recovery integrity\n858\t\n859\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n860\t\n861\t### Requirement\n862\t\n863\tIf logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n864\t\n865\t  Why this is hard: Not every outside API supports idempotency keys or a reliable status check. A crash between a successful remote action and the local checkpoint can make replay repeat that action. A local table of past requests doesn't close that gap.\n866\t\n867\t  If you can't fully do it: Use idempotency enforced by the destination where it exists. Write a durable operation record before sending. If the result is unclear, pause replay and reconcile using the destination's evidence or an operator. Don't retry blindly. Turn off automatic replay for side effects that don't support it, and document that limit. Reject checkpoints you can't verify, and recover from a trusted state. Manual recovery doesn't prove automatic replay is safe.\n868\t\n869\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Manual recovery, slower completion, and no automatic replay for unsupported integrations. Never call blind retries of unclear, permanent actions \"safe replay.\" Remaining recovery-control gaps need allowed G2 acceptance.\n870\t\n871\t### Verification recipe\n872\t\n873\tChange a copy of a checkpoint or audit record. Recovery must reject it. Also cut off, roll back, and replace the chain or checkpoint. Verification must use a separately protected trust anchor. For integrations that support safe automatic replay, replay a good checkpoint twice. The action must happen once, with the duplicate recorded as blocked. For integrations without that support, inject an unclear result. Check that replay pauses for reconciliation with the destination and sends no duplicate.\n874\t\n875\t**Evidence to keep:** Integrity check result, idempotency results enforced by the destination, action counts, manual-recovery hold and outcome.\n876\t\n877\t### Checks for likely_pass\n878\t\n879\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n880\t\n881\t- **R14.1** Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n882\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n883\t- **R14.2** Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n884\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n885\t- **R14.3** Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n886\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n887\t- **R14.4** Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n888\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n889\t- **R14.5** When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n890\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n891\t\n892\t### Not covered here\n893\t\n894\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n895\t\n896\t- What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop → **R12**\n897\t- Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested → **C07**\n898\t- Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n899\t\n900\t### Heuristics\n901\t\n902\t**Provable from code:** partly\n903\t\n904\t**Look for:**\n905\t- Checkpoints and recovery logs signed or hash-chained, and verified against a separately protected key or trust anchor before use.\n906\t- Checks that catch truncation, rollback to an older checkpoint, and replacement of a whole chain (for example a signed head or sequence number stored elsewhere).\n907\t- Idempotency keys sent to destinations that support them, for example `Idempotency-Key` headers.\n908\t- A durable operation record written before sending, and replay that pauses for reconciliation when a result is unclear.\n909\t\n910\t**Red flags:**\n911\t- LangGraph or similar checkpoints loaded from a writable store with no integrity check.\n912\t- Hash chain verified only against itself, with no outside anchor.\n913\t- Automatic replay or retry of payments, emails, or other permanent actions with no idempotency key.\n914\t- A local 'already sent' table used as the only duplicate guard.\n915\t\n916\t**Where to look:**\n917\t- Checkpointer and resume code\n918\t- Retry and replay logic in tool clients\n919\t- Audit log storage and signing setup\n920\t\n921\t**Needs runtime evidence:** That changed, truncated, rolled-back, and replaced checkpoints are rejected, and replays happen once or pause. Recipe R14.\n922\t\n923\t**N/A when:** Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\n924\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"b1beeeb3-c9ea-4f58-9fba-b8bac3aa3f01","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:37.109Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/run-time.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# R — Run-time\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## R01 · G2 · C5 — Validate every input boundary, including API responses\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n\n  Why this is hard: An API response can be well-formed, correctly signed, and still carry harmful instructions. Free text and streamed responses are hard to inspect fully. Passing a schema check proves the structure, not that the content is safe.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with type checks, size limits, sender checks, and rejecting broken content. Add content checks for attack patterns you've seen. Buffer or limit streams when the required checks can't run before use. Keep permissions narrow, and use R02 to contain instructions that slip through. Record which integrations and payload types you tested and which you didn't.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Buffering delay, rejecting some valid responses, or supporting fewer payload types. Incomplete detection of attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 risk acceptance. That needs tested containment and clear limits on what was covered. Schema checks alone don't fully protect against injection.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHave a mock integration return wrong types, oversized bodies, hostile text, error responses, and streamed chunks. Where signatures apply, send a callback with a bad signature. Bad payloads must be rejected or held aside before the model sees them or anything runs them. Turn off a required check and confirm the system fails closed.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fixtures for each integration, validation decisions, checks of what was passed on.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R01.1** Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code.\n- **R01.2** Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit.\n- **R01.3** Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues.\n- **R01.4** Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks.\n- **R01.5** Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked → **R02**\n- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n- Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check → **E07**\n- Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs → **R03**\n- Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus → **R04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A list of every input source: user input, API responses (errors and streams), tool and MCP output, webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n- Schema and type checks on tool and API results before they reach the model, for example Pydantic or Zod models, JSON Schema, or OpenAI Agents SDK output types.\n- Size limits on bodies, files, and streamed chunks, enforced before content is used.\n- Sender checks: webhook signature verification (for example HMAC with constant-time compare), mTLS, or token checks on callbacks.\n- A content check for injection or attack payloads (for example an input guardrail or scanner) with a reject or quarantine path.\n- Fail-closed handling: if a validator errors or times out, the content is dropped or held, not passed on.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Raw `response.text` or tool output passed straight into messages with no type, size, or content check.\n- Webhook handlers that parse the body without verifying a signature, or compare signatures with `==`.\n- `try: validate(x) except Exception: pass` or similar code that forwards content when a check fails.\n- Content from internal services or authenticated APIs trusted by default with no checks.\n- Streamed responses fed token by token to the model or a tool with no limit or buffering.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tool wrappers and MCP client code\n- HTTP clients, webhook and callback handlers\n- Retrieval and file-loading code\n- Guardrail config (OpenAI Agents SDK input guardrails, NeMo Guardrails, Claude Agent SDK `PostToolUse` hooks)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That hostile, oversized, faked, and broken payloads from each integration are rejected, and that disabling a check fails closed. Recipe R01 uses mock integrations.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R02 · G2 · C5 — Separate data from instructions and test injection containment\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nKeep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n\n  Why this is hard: A model may follow an instruction hidden in normal-looking data, even with separate channels. A fixed set of injection tests can't prove that every injection will fail. Model behavior can also change from run to run.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Assume some injections will work. Send the forbidden calls they would cause straight to your credential limits, egress rules, and action gates. Also run full end-to-end examples. Limit the tools and data the agent can reach. When containment is uncertain, make high-impact actions draft-only, or require approval for each one. Report structure checks, injection detection, and blocked side effects as separate results.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer finished tasks, fewer tools, and more approvals, each for one specific action. Remaining detection gaps can get G2 acceptance with clear exposure and separate proof of containment. The required access and destructive-action gates still apply.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHide an instruction to send a fake secret to your test destination. Put it inside a schema-valid API response, document, or tool result. Inspect the model request that gets built, and check that data and instructions are kept apart. Also send the forbidden tool call this would cause straight to enforcement. No secret may reach the destination. Repeat each fixture a set number of times, and add adaptive versions. Report the success rate per attempt and across all attempts, grouped by how serious the action is. Detector results don't count as proof of containment.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Model request with role and source labels, attempt counts and success rates, gate denial, destination receipt log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R02.1** Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\n- **R02.2** Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself.\n- **R02.3** Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output.\n- **R02.4** Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it.\n- **R02.5** Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n- Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas → **E04**\n- One run planting instructions in another run's memory → **R05**\n- Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing → **R03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tool output and outside content placed in tool-result or user-role messages, never in the system or developer message.\n- Source and trust labels on content, for example wrapping in tagged blocks with origin metadata.\n- Containment in code that doesn't depend on the model: narrow credentials (B02), tool allowlists (B01), egress rules (B09), and destructive-action gates (B05).\n- Injection tests that repeat each attack and include adaptive versions, with results reported as structure checks, detection, and blocked side effects separately.\n- High-impact actions draft-only or approval-gated where containment is uncertain.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Untrusted tool output concatenated into the system message or a policy prompt.\n- Injection detection (a classifier, regex, or guardrail model) treated as the whole defense; detection alone is not containment.\n- Retrieved documents or web pages formatted as instructions ('Follow these steps: ...') in the prompt.\n- Injection tests run once each with no repeats or adaptive variants.\n- The agent can read untrusted content and also send data out with no gate between them.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Prompt and message-building code\n- Retrieval and tool-result formatting\n- Red-team or injection test suites\n- Action gates, credential scopes, and egress config that contain a followed injection\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Success rates of planted injections, and proof that forbidden calls are blocked at enforcement. Recipe R02: plant an exfiltration instruction in schema-valid content, repeat with adaptive versions, and check the destination log.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R03 · G2 · C5/C9 — Prevent output and log leakage\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Data and privacy, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRemove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nReturn an easy-to-spot fake secret in tool output, debug output, and an API error. Trigger a normal run. Search model inputs, normal logs, and outside responses for the marker. It must be removed or properly restricted before it reaches any of them. Get the agent to produce a Markdown image or link whose URL carries the marker to your test destination. The display client must not fetch it. Also feed generated SQL, shell, and HTML payloads into the systems that use them. Check that parameterized queries and escaping stop the payloads from running.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Marker test, redaction decisions, searches of each output path.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R03.1** Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction.\n- **R03.2** The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo.\n- **R03.3** Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped.\n- **R03.4** Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination → **B09**\n- Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images → **B03**\n- Incoming hostile payloads not validated → **R01**\n- Retrieval returning another tenant's documents → **R04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Redaction of secrets and sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or errors reach the model, logs, or outside recipients (for example log filters, OpenTelemetry processors that drop or mask attributes).\n- Parameterized SQL (`cursor.execute(sql, params)`), argument lists for shell calls, and context-correct escaping for HTML.\n- Markdown images and link previews blocked or limited to approved URLs in the client that shows agent output (for example a CSP `img-src` allowlist or a sanitizer that strips external images).\n- Tests with fake secret markers searched across model inputs, logs, and outside responses.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `subprocess(..., shell=True)` or `os.system` with model-generated text.\n- SQL built with f-strings, `+`, or `.format()` from model output.\n- Model output rendered as HTML with `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, or `|safe` without sanitizing.\n- Full prompts, tool payloads, or exception traces with credentials logged at INFO, or OpenTelemetry content capture (for example `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`) on with no redaction.\n- Chat UI renders Markdown images from any URL.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Shell, SQL, and code-execution tools\n- Logging config and telemetry exporters\n- Chat UI rendering code and CSP headers\n- Error handlers that return messages to the model or user\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That the fake-secret marker doesn't appear in any output path and the client doesn't fetch marker URLs. Recipe R03.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R04 · G2 · C5/C6 — Retrieval-corpus integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Infrastructure, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nThe retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n\n  Why this is hard: Removing a document from the index doesn't remove copies in caches, running contexts, summaries, or memory. Permission changes can take time to spread. And if the model leaves a document out of its answer, that doesn't prove search excluded it.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Set a limit you can measure, such as \"no revoked chunks in new search results after N minutes.\" Check current access at search time, clear caches, and stop or restart affected runs when needed. If you can't find all affected copies, quarantine the whole collection and rebuild it from approved sources. Record copies that remain and how long they were exposed. Don't claim you removed every derived copy.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A stated revocation delay that fits the risk, search downtime, or less search coverage while you rebuild. Record the exposure during the delay and any G2 exception. Where access must end right away, block the affected searches and runs until revocation takes effect.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCreate searchable test documents for tenants A and B, each with a unique marker. Try to add content with an unapproved identity. It must be denied. Search as tenant A and confirm no tenant B chunks come back. Then quarantine a poisoned test document, expire an old one, and revoke access to a third. After the set propagation time, searches for the exact markers, meaning-based searches, and cache checks must return none of the affected chunks.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Permissions for adding content, and the rejection; each chunk's source, time, trust, and access records; searches before and after; cache-clearing timestamps.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R04.1** Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check.\n- **R04.2** Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths.\n- **R04.3** Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument.\n- **R04.4** Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants → **R05**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions → **R01**\n- Embedding or reranker model swapped without review → **C05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Write access to the index limited to named ingestion identities and sources.\n- Per-chunk metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions (for example in vector-store metadata fields).\n- Tenant access enforced in the search query itself, for example a required metadata filter or a separate namespace or collection per tenant.\n- Code paths to quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and to clear search caches.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- One shared vector collection for all tenants with no tenant filter on search.\n- Tenant filter applied after retrieval, or by the model, instead of in the query.\n- The agent itself can write to the index (for example an `add_documents` tool) with no review.\n- Chunks stored with no source or access metadata, so they can't be found or revoked later.\n- Search results cached with no invalidation on revoke.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Ingestion pipelines and loaders\n- Vector-store client code (Pinecone, Weaviate, pgvector, Chroma, OpenSearch)\n- Retriever setup (for example LangChain retriever `search_kwargs` filters)\n- Cache layers in front of search\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That cross-tenant searches return nothing and that revoked, expired, or quarantined chunks leave search results and caches within the stated time. Recipe R04.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\n\n## R05 · G3 · C6 — Memory isolation and write validation\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSeparate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n\n  Why this is hard: A saved preference or summary can act as an instruction in a later session. Write checks can't always tell a useful memory from a harmful one, especially when agents share memory across tasks.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with memory per task or per instance, and turn sharing off by default. Allow only defined memory fields. Require review before an entry moves into shared, long-lived memory. If a store can't keep memory separate, turn off saved writes or sharing for that workflow. Test the reduced setup, and record any feature you turned off to get there.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less personalization, repeated work, or shared memory that only holds reviewed entries. Turning off saved writes or sharing can reduce exposure. Any remaining memory-control gap can be deferred only where G3 allows.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nIn one instance, try to save a harmful instruction, and try to read and write another instance's, type's, or tenant's test memory. Confirm the write checks and scope blocks work. Start a new session and confirm rejected content wasn't saved or reused. Send a poisoning fixture through ordinary queries only. Confirm the agent's own output doesn't move into trusted memory without review, and that unverified entries expire.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Memory policy, rejected writes and reads, new-session context.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R05.1** Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace.\n- **R05.2** Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues.\n- **R05.3** The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved.\n- **R05.4** Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it → **R06**\n- Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant → **R04**\n- Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API → **B02**\n- Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Memory keys or namespaces that include tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed (for example LangGraph store namespaces, per-thread checkpointers).\n- Validation on memory writes, including content that arrives through ordinary queries, with only defined fields allowed.\n- A review step before agent output moves into shared or long-lived memory.\n- Expiry (TTL) on unverified entries, and sharing off by default.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- One global memory store or key with no tenant or instance scope.\n- The agent writes free text to persistent memory through a tool with no checks.\n- Summaries of the agent's own output saved as trusted memory with no review.\n- Memory entries that never expire.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory or store modules, LangGraph `BaseStore`/checkpointer setup, Mem0 or similar config\n- Memory write tools\n- Session and thread handling\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a poisoned run can't plant instructions in another run's memory and that cross-scope reads and writes fail. Recipe R05.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n\n## R06 · G3 · C6 — Memory provenance and cleanup\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Data and privacy.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n\n  Why this is hard: Summaries and other derived memories can lose their link to the source that tainted them. Deleting one known entry can leave related content in use. Tracking each entry's source can't recover history that was never recorded.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep source and writer links on new entries and on summaries made from them. When the history is incomplete, quarantine or reset the affected memory area and its caches. Restart affected sessions and rebuild from known sources. Record the wider reset and the useful memory it cost. Keep missing provenance as a Gap until you can show it. A cleanup alone does not restore it.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Losing useful memories, and the cost of resetting a wider area when a precise cleanup isn't possible. Missing provenance stays a G3 gap. Record what was thrown away and how trusted memory will be rebuilt.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSeed an approved test entry with a known source. Flag it as tainted, find related entries by source or writer, and quarantine or delete them. Later sessions must not retrieve them. Confirm any derived memory or cache is also handled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Provenance query, cleanup log, later search results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R06.1** Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths.\n- **R06.2** Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links.\n- **R06.3** Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place.\n- **R06.4** Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task → **R05**\n- Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired → **R04**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Each memory entry stores writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n- Summaries and derived entries keep links to their sources.\n- Code or runbooks to find entries by source or writer, quarantine or delete them, and clear related caches.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Memory rows with only content and a timestamp, no writer or source fields.\n- Summaries that replace source entries and drop their links.\n- No delete or quarantine function for memory, or one that skips caches and derived entries.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Memory schema or data model\n- Summarization and compaction code\n- Admin or cleanup scripts\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a tainted entry and its related entries can be found and removed, and later sessions don't retrieve them. Recipe R06.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\n\n## R07 · G2 · Obs-T2 — Decision-time context size\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCapture decisions with short and long contexts, and a tool handoff. Compare the context sizes logged with the harness's documented counting method and timing, including units and any estimate limits. Include cases with cached input and compacted context. Where available, record compaction with the OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute. If that attribute is missing, it means unknown, not false. Every action must have a searchable record, linked to its trace, of the value and how it was measured. Unknown context must be flagged, never reported as zero.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Counting method, model-request metadata, trace query.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R07.1** Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action.\n- **R07.2** Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`).\n- **R07.3** The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction.\n- **R07.4** The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Behavior not compared across context-size ranges → **R09**\n- Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Context size in tokens recorded on each action span or log record, for example OpenTelemetry `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` plus a BRACE context-size attribute per `otel-conventions.md`.\n- A documented counting method that covers cached input and compaction, and marks each value as measured, estimated, or unavailable.\n- Compaction recorded, for example the `gen_ai.conversation.compacted` attribute.\n- The value carried across tool handoffs and stored next to the action's identity and result.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No token or context-size field on action records.\n- Missing values written as `0` instead of null or 'unknown'.\n- Per-call billing totals used as context size with no stated method showing they match.\n- Context size logged per run only, not per action.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and span attributes\n- Model-call wrappers and usage handling\n- Compaction or summarization code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That logged values match the counting method across short, long, cached, and compacted contexts, and are searchable per action. Recipe R07.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R08 · G3 · C7 — Security monitoring\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nKeep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend each configured security event through test traffic. Check that the rule fires and reaches the security responder within the target time. Run a harmless control case too, to tell a working detector from an alert that always fires.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Rule versions, event fixtures, alert and delivery timestamps.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R08.1** Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo.\n- **R08.2** Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire.\n- **R08.3** Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n- No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Security detection rules kept apart from answer-quality evals, for example SIEM rules, Falco rules, or alert definitions in the repo.\n- Rules for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n- Denials and policy decisions emitted as events that rules can match.\n- Alert routing to a named responder (for example PagerDuty or Opsgenie routing config).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only quality or eval monitoring (LangSmith, Langfuse, Phoenix scores) with no security rules.\n- Denied tool calls logged at DEBUG or not logged at all.\n- Alerts sent to a shared channel with no named owner.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection-as-code repos, SIEM or Falco rule files\n- Alerting and on-call routing config\n- Policy-decision logging in the harness\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each rule fires on test traffic, stays quiet on a harmless control, and reaches the responder in time. Recipe R08. Rules often live outside the agent repo.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R09 · G3 · C7/Obs-T2 — Sequence and context-aware detection\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTest suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n\n  Why this is hard: Real research and data theft can look alike step by step. Real attack examples may be rare. A detector tuned only to test cases can miss real behavior, or flood responders with false alarms.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Start with a few clear signals. Examples: an unusual number of reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, too many sub-agents, or a used-up budget. Measure how many seeded attacks you catch, how many false alarms you raise, and how fast alerts arrive, for each context-size range. Use hard rate and cost limits, and limit destinations, while you improve detection. Say exactly which sequences you cover and which you miss. Basic rules don't prove general sequence detection.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A narrower, written detection scope, and a measured false-alarm rate that responders can handle. For lower-stakes deployments, defer broader coverage under G3 while keeping hard limits. Where G3 blocks release, reduce the real exposure or finish the required coverage.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nDefine a labeled harmless sequence and a labeled abusive one, made of calls that are each allowed. An example is too many reads followed by an attempt to send data out. Replay both at the set small and large context sizes. Record catches and false alarms for each size range, against thresholds set before the run. If you screen actions against the original task, include a hijacked sequence that uses only allowed tools. Confirm the checker doesn't receive the untrusted content.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Labeled sequences, context ranges, thresholds, results for each range.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R09.1** Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event.\n- **R09.2** Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered.\n- **R09.3** Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts.\n- **R09.4** If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected → **R08**\n- No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent → **R10**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Detection over sequences of allowed actions, for example many reads followed by an outside send, repeated denials, unusual fan-out, or a used-up budget.\n- Thresholds or baselines split by context-size range.\n- A pre-action checker that compares high-impact actions with the original task and does not receive the untrusted content in between.\n- Labeled harmless and abusive sequences used to measure catches and false alarms.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only per-call rules; nothing looks at chains of actions.\n- One fleet-wide baseline with no split by context size.\n- A task-alignment checker given the full conversation, including untrusted tool output.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Detection rules and analytics jobs\n- Pre-execution hooks or guardrails\n- Detection test fixtures\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Catch and false-alarm rates per context-size range against thresholds set before the run. Recipe R09.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R10 · G3 · C7 — Misaligned-behavior review\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Governance.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIf deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n\n  Why this is hard: Actions don't reliably reveal hidden intent. A second model can share the first model's blind spots. Catching labeled evasive examples doesn't prove you will catch deliberate misalignment.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Make narrow claims about behavior you can see. An example is catching attempts to split a forbidden operation across several calls. Keep a record of misses and false positives. Rely on separate permission limits, action approvals, and stop controls for containment. Where detection falls short, reduce autonomy or remove high-impact powers. Record an unproven detector as a gap. Never label the model's intent as safe.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Uncertainty about hidden intent, as long as claims stay limited to measured behavior. This checklist does not certify alignment. Less autonomy and more review are also fine. Missing required behavior detection still falls under G3.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nBuild a labeled review set. Include harmless traces and traces with attempted evasion, such as splitting a forbidden operation across calls. Run the reviewer or detector, and record misses and false alarms against the pass criteria you set. Include traces from a red-team agent that was told it is being watched. Record what the reviewer could see and which reasoning was hidden from it. This tests catching visible behavior. It doesn't reveal the model's hidden intent.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Review-set version, detector or model version, results and stated limits.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate → **B05**\n- A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure → **A07**\n- Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected → **R09**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- A second-model or pattern reviewer of actions and arguments, with a written record of what it can see, including whether reasoning is visible.\n- A labeled review set with evasive traces (for example a forbidden operation split across calls) and red-team traces from an agent told it is watched.\n- Rule-based access and action controls that stay in place alongside the reviewer.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- A reviewer model used in place of rule-based permission or approval controls.\n- Claims that the model's intent is safe, with no measured misses or false positives.\n- Reviewer sees only the final answer, not actions and arguments.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Monitor or reviewer agent code and prompts\n- Red-team and evaluation results\n- Threat model stating whether deliberate misalignment is in scope\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Measured misses and false alarms on a labeled evasive set, plus red-team runs. Code can only show a reviewer exists. Recipe R10.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\n\n## R11 · G1 · C8 — Tested kill switch\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nName who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStart a controlled run and trigger the operator's stop control. Record the time of the last allowed action and the final stop. Repeat while a tool call is in progress. Have the agent use its own credentials and tools to try to disable, change, or delay the stop path. It must fail. All work must stop within the time limit. Use E09 for sub-agents and their children.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Drill trace, operator action, measured stop time.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R11.1** A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped.\n- **R11.2** The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal).\n- **R11.3** Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues.\n- **R11.4** The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find streams, subscriptions, and background tasks (`asyncio.create_task`, goroutines, websockets, SSE). 2) Check the stop handler closes them. 3) Answer no if any is left open, detached, or untracked.\n- **R11.5** Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who can set the stop (auth on the endpoint, IAM on the flag store). 2) Answer no if any authenticated user or service can set it with no named operator list. Answer unknown if access lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers → **E09**\n- In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop → **R12**\n- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A stop path outside the agent's control, for example an operator-only flag in a separate service, scaling the deployment to zero, revoking the agent identity, or a gateway kill rule.\n- The agent's credentials can't write to the stop flag or its config (check IAM and RBAC).\n- The harness checks the stop signal before each action and cancels running tool calls, streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n- A written list of who may stop the agent and a maximum stop time.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Stop works by sending the agent a message or prompt asking it to halt.\n- Stop flag stored where the agent can write, for example the same database, config map, or memory it uses.\n- Stop checked only between turns, with no cancel for in-flight tool calls or streams.\n- Sub-agents or background jobs not reached by the stop signal.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Runner loop and cancellation handling\n- Ops scripts and runbooks for stopping the agent\n- IAM/RBAC for the stop control\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** A dated drill on the current deployment with measured stop time, including an in-progress tool call and an agent attempt to block the stop. Recipe R11. Sub-agents are covered by E09.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R12 · G1 · C8 — Safe state after stopping\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Operations, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nDecide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n\n  Why this is hard: An outside action may already be done when the stop arrives. Emails can't reliably be unsent. Payments, writes, and queued jobs each cancel and reconcile differently.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: For each operation, record whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward. Stop new work from being sent, turn off retries, check the destination's state, and send unclear results to a named operator. For operations with no workable recovery path, remove autonomous execution or use an approved draft-then-commit flow. Measure stopping new work separately from resolving work already done. Stopping the process alone does not pass this check.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Slower manual reconciliation and fewer autonomous actions. Some completed effects may need reconciling rather than reversing, following the written safe-state procedure. Untracked effects, or work that keeps its authority past the stop time limit, still block release.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStop runs before a write, during a transaction, and after a finished outside test action. Check the queues and the destination's state. Each case must end canceled, completed, or reconciled. Or it must be on a tracked hold for reconciliation, with an owner, no dependent actions, and no blind retries. No unexplained orphaned work or restart is acceptable.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Outcome of each scenario, queue snapshots, undo and reconciliation records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R12.1** For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a per-operation stop policy (cancel, complete, reconcile). 2) Answer no if any operation type the agent can run has no stated stop behavior.\n- **R12.2** On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue and retry code (`retry`, `backoff`, queue workers). 2) Check stop cancels or holds queued items and turns retries off. 3) Answer no if retries continue after stop or queued jobs still run.\n- **R12.3** Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find handling of unclear or in-progress outside actions at stop. 2) Check they go on a tracked hold with an owner and block dependent actions. 3) Answer no if they are dropped, retried, or dependents proceed.\n- **R12.4** A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find restart logic (supervisors, `restartPolicy`, cron, auto-resume). 2) Answer no if a stopped run restarts automatically (`restartPolicy: Always`, auto-resume from checkpoint) without an operator action.\n- **R12.5** Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find workers and queued jobs and the credentials they hold. 2) Check stop revokes or expires them. 3) Answer no if workers keep long-lived credentials after stop, or jobs carry their own tokens that stay valid.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop path depends on the agent or has no tested stop time → **R11**\n- Recovery replay repeating irreversible actions or using tampered checkpoints → **R14**\n- Rollback restoring revoked permissions → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A per-operation record of whether it can be blocked, canceled, or only reconciled afterward.\n- On stop: queues drained or held, retries turned off, and in-flight transactions rolled back.\n- Reconcile or compensate code for outside side effects, and unclear results sent to a named operator.\n- Restart requires an operator; no auto-restart policy resumes agent work.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Kubernetes `restartPolicy: Always` or a supervisor that resumes the agent's queue after a stop with no human step.\n- Queued jobs or scheduled tasks that keep their credentials and run after the stop.\n- Retries with backoff on outside actions that keep going during or after a stop.\n- Stop handled only by killing the process.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Job queues, workers, and schedulers\n- Shutdown and signal handlers\n- Retry config and compensation code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Outcomes of stops before a write, mid-transaction, and after an outside action, with queue and destination snapshots. Recipe R12.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R13 · G1 · C9 — Full execution audit\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRecord each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n\n  Why this is hard: Agent logs usually show what the agent tried. The destination system knows whether it actually happened. A timeout can mean the call failed, or it worked and the reply was lost. So a local trace can look complete and still have an unknown result.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Give each operation an ID before you send it. Keep receipts or status checks from the destination where you can. Match attempts against the destination's records, and mark unresolved results clearly. Pause dependent actions and retries until each one is resolved. If an integration can't give you enough evidence to trace and recover, limit or remove that path. Sampling and the agent's own reports can't replace the required action record.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Pausing dependent work while an unclear result gets resolved. Keep the attempt, the uncertainty, and the final answer in the graph. Less automation is a fair cost. Missing or made-up action evidence is not a G1 pass.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRun a known sequence with an allowed call, a denial, an approval, a tool error, and a retry. Confirm each decision record names the policy version, and each approved action names its approval ID. Rebuild the graph using only stored audit records. Compare it with the test runner's and the destination's logs. Every expected action must be accounted for. Unresolved results at the destination must be marked clearly, with dependent work paused until they are reconciled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Expected event list, rebuilt graph, query for missing events.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R13.1** Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find step logging (trace spans, audit log writes). 2) Check each record has run ID, proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or logging happens only on the success branch.\n- **R13.2** Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization decision code. 2) Check both allow and deny are logged with the policy version. 3) Answer no if denials are not logged, or no policy version is recorded.\n- **R13.3** Approved actions record their approval ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where approved actions are logged. 2) Answer no if the approval ID is not recorded with the action.\n- **R13.4** Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find error, timeout, and retry handling. 2) Check each is logged and each operation gets an ID before sending. 3) Answer no if `except`/`catch` blocks skip logging, retries are not logged, or the ID is created after the response.\n- **R13.5** Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find who writes records. 2) Answer no if the model writes them via a tool, records are built from the final answer, or logging runs only if the run completes.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A linked trace per run (for example OpenTelemetry spans with parent links) covering proposed actions, tool calls with key arguments, authorization decisions with policy version, denials, approvals with IDs, results, errors, and timestamps.\n- An operation ID assigned before each outside call, and destination receipts or status checks stored.\n- Unresolved results marked clearly, with dependent actions paused.\n- Audit records written to a store the agent can't change.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only the final answer or chat transcript is stored.\n- Denials and approval decisions not logged, or logged without the policy version.\n- Trace sampling (for example `TraceIdRatioBased` below 1.0) applied to the action audit.\n- Timeouts logged as failures with no marker that the result is unknown.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tracing and logging setup, span attributes\n- Policy and approval decision code\n- Audit storage config and retention\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That a known run with an allowed call, denial, approval, error, and retry can be rebuilt from stored records alone and matches destination logs. Recipe R13.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## R14 · G2 · C9 — Recovery integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIf logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n\n  Why this is hard: Not every outside API supports idempotency keys or a reliable status check. A crash between a successful remote action and the local checkpoint can make replay repeat that action. A local table of past requests doesn't close that gap.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use idempotency enforced by the destination where it exists. Write a durable operation record before sending. If the result is unclear, pause replay and reconcile using the destination's evidence or an operator. Don't retry blindly. Turn off automatic replay for side effects that don't support it, and document that limit. Reject checkpoints you can't verify, and recover from a trusted state. Manual recovery doesn't prove automatic replay is safe.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Manual recovery, slower completion, and no automatic replay for unsupported integrations. Never call blind retries of unclear, permanent actions \"safe replay.\" Remaining recovery-control gaps need allowed G2 acceptance.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nChange a copy of a checkpoint or audit record. Recovery must reject it. Also cut off, roll back, and replace the chain or checkpoint. Verification must use a separately protected trust anchor. For integrations that support safe automatic replay, replay a good checkpoint twice. The action must happen once, with the duplicate recorded as blocked. For integrations without that support, inject an unclear result. Check that replay pauses for reconciliation with the destination and sends no duplicate.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Integrity check result, idempotency results enforced by the destination, action counts, manual-recovery hold and outcome.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **R14.1** Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find checkpoint and audit storage used for recovery. 2) Check for hash chaining or signatures. 3) Answer no if records are plain rows or files with no chain or signature.\n- **R14.2** Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find recovery code that loads checkpoints. 2) Check it verifies against a key stored separately and refuses failures. 3) Answer no if the key sits in the same store, verification is skipped, or failure is logged and recovery continues.\n- **R14.3** Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check verification uses a signed head or sequence number held outside the store. 2) Answer no if truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, or replacing the whole chain would still pass.\n- **R14.4** Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay code. 2) Check it sends idempotency keys (`Idempotency-Key`, `client_token`) only to integrations that enforce them. 3) Answer no if replay sends without keys, uses a local dedupe table as the only guard, or replays to integrations without support.\n- **R14.5** When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find replay handling for unclear results or missing idempotency. 2) Answer no if replay resends instead of pausing for reconciliation, or retry is the default on timeout.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- What happens to in-flight writes and queued actions at stop → **R12**\n- Rollback to an approved earlier release not tested → **C07**\n- Agent able to delete or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Checkpoints and recovery logs signed or hash-chained, and verified against a separately protected key or trust anchor before use.\n- Checks that catch truncation, rollback to an older checkpoint, and replacement of a whole chain (for example a signed head or sequence number stored elsewhere).\n- Idempotency keys sent to destinations that support them, for example `Idempotency-Key` headers.\n- A durable operation record written before sending, and replay that pauses for reconciliation when a result is unclear.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- LangGraph or similar checkpoints loaded from a writable store with no integrity check.\n- Hash chain verified only against itself, with no outside anchor.\n- Automatic replay or retry of payments, emails, or other permanent actions with no idempotency key.\n- A local 'already sent' table used as the only duplicate guard.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Checkpointer and resume code\n- Retry and replay logic in tool clients\n- Audit log storage and signing setup\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That changed, truncated, rolled-back, and replaced checkpoints are rejected, and replays happen once or pause. Recipe R14.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\n","numLines":924,"startLine":1,"totalLines":924}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfddpQHVT4EmChNY8eiYR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016GbkQjLU6g47LXLj8c9GK9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/agent.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27176,"cache_read_input_tokens":118384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27176},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"1703dfd3-14a8-450e-98cb-3c89c903564a","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:39.206Z","request_id":"req_011CfddpPyPFUaG6irgi5stv","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016GbkQjLU6g47LXLj8c9GK9":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/agent.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016GbkQjLU6g47LXLj8c9GK9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# A — Agent\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## A01 · G1 · C2/Obs-T1 — Distinct agent identity\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tGive the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n14\t\n15\t### Verification recipe\n16\t\n17\tLaunch the agent from a user account. Look at the identity (principal) the agent was issued, and compare the user's permissions with the agent's. Revoke only the agent and try again. Its identity and access must be separate from the user's and from unrelated agents.\n18\t\n19\t**Evidence to keep:** Identity bindings, permission comparison, revocation results.\n20\t\n21\t### Checks for likely_pass\n22\t\n23\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n24\t\n25\t- **A01.1** The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n26\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n27\t- **A01.2** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n28\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n29\t- **A01.3** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n30\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n31\t- **A01.4** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n32\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n33\t\n34\t### Not covered here\n35\t\n36\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n37\t\n38\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n39\t- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n40\t- A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed → **B02**\n41\t- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\n42\t\n43\t### Heuristics\n44\t\n45\t**Provable from code:** partly\n46\t\n47\t**Look for:**\n48\t- The agent runs as its own workload identity: a dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccount, cloud IAM role (AWS IAM role, GCP service account, Azure managed identity), SPIFFE ID, or Entra Agent ID, not shared with other services.\n49\t- Tool and API clients get credentials from that identity (workload identity federation, IRSA, token exchange per RFC 8693) instead of from the user session.\n50\t- When a user starts a run, the user is passed as a delegated subject (for example an `act`/`sub` pair in an exchanged token), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n51\t- Permissions are attached to the agent principal in IaC (Terraform IAM policies, RBAC RoleBindings) and are narrower than the launching user's.\n52\t- A documented way to revoke or disable the agent principal alone (delete role binding, disable service principal, rotate its key).\n53\t\n54\t**Red flags:**\n55\t- Agent calls downstream APIs with the launching user's OAuth access token or session cookie, forwarded as-is.\n56\t- One service account, API key, or IAM role is shared by several unrelated agents or by the agent and the main app backend.\n57\t- Long-lived static API keys for the agent in env files or code, with no per-agent principal behind them.\n58\t- Agent identity is only a string label in config or a prompt (for example `agent_name: support-bot`) with no credential bound to it.\n59\t- Revoking access would require disabling the user or a shared credential, because there is no agent-only principal.\n60\t\n61\t**Where to look:**\n62\t- Kubernetes ServiceAccount, RoleBinding, and pod spec `serviceAccountName`\n63\t- Terraform/CloudFormation/Bicep IAM roles, policies, and identity federation\n64\t- auth and credential setup in the harness and tool clients (token exchange, OAuth client config)\n65\t- .env files, secret manager references, and Helm values\n66\t\n67\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show how identity is wired, not that the issued principal and its live permissions differ from the user's, or that revoking only the agent works. Needs the A01 recipe: launch from a user account, compare permissions, revoke the agent alone, and retry.\n68\t\n69\t**N/A when:** never\n70\t\n71\t## A02 · G1 · Obs-T1 — All six identity fields\n72\t\n73\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Business owner, Governance.\n74\t\n75\t### Requirement\n76\t\n77\tCheck that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n78\t\n79\t### Verification recipe\n80\t\n81\tQuery every record from a controlled run with successful, denied, failed, async, and background actions. Count missing or empty values for each of the six fields. The count must be zero. Confirm the values match the known run and tenant. Confirm tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage or tracestate headers, which have no integrity protection.\n82\t\n83\t**Evidence to keep:** Field-completeness query, counts, sample records.\n84\t\n85\t### Checks for likely_pass\n86\t\n87\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n88\t\n89\t- **A02.1** The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n90\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n91\t- **A02.2** All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n92\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n93\t- **A02.3** Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n94\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n95\t- **A02.4** Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n96\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n97\t\n98\t### Not covered here\n99\t\n100\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n101\t\n102\t- Identity labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n103\t- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n104\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n105\t- Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt → **A06**\n106\t\n107\t### Heuristics\n108\t\n109\t**Provable from code:** partly\n110\t\n111\t**Look for:**\n112\t- Telemetry or audit code sets all six fields on every action: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, and W3C trace context (`traceparent`).\n113\t- Fields are set in one shared place (span processor, logging middleware, audit wrapper around tool dispatch) so every tool call inherits them.\n114\t- Denied, failed, retried, async, and background paths (queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks) also emit the fields, not just the main request handler.\n115\t- Tenant and owner values come from verified identity (token claims, IAM tags, SPIFFE path) in the trusted layer.\n116\t- A test or query that counts missing or empty identity fields per record.\n117\t\n118\t**Red flags:**\n119\t- Identity attributes are added only on the success path; `except`/`catch` blocks and policy-denial branches log without them.\n120\t- Background jobs or queue consumers start new traces with no tenant, instance, or type ID.\n121\t- Tenant or owner is read from OTel baggage or `tracestate`, which have no integrity protection.\n122\t- `agent.type.id` or `agent.instance.id` never appears anywhere in the code or config.\n123\t- `gen_ai.agent.id` is overwritten with a per-run ID and used as the only agent identifier.\n124\t\n125\t**Where to look:**\n126\t- OpenTelemetry setup: span processors, resource attributes, instrumentation config\n127\t- tool dispatch and policy-check code, including error and deny branches\n128\t- queue workers, schedulers, webhook handlers\n129\t- audit log writer and log schema definitions\n130\t\n131\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't prove every emitted record is complete or that values match the real run and tenant. Needs the A02 recipe: query all records from a controlled run with success, deny, failure, async, and background actions and show zero missing values.\n132\t\n133\t**N/A when:** never\n134\t\n135\t## A03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Content-derived type identity\n136\t\n137\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team.\n138\t\n139\t### Requirement\n140\t\n141\tCompute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n142\t\n143\t  Why this is hard: A hash only covers the inputs you can see. It can't prove that a hosted model's hidden weights or training stayed the same behind the same name.\n144\t\n145\t  If you can't fully do it: Hash the exact local artifacts and the model, checkpoint, and adapter references you have, using a manifest anyone can rebuild. Record the model name you asked for separately from the version the provider says it served. Mark hidden information clearly. State that the ID fingerprints this recorded setup. Handle changes behind remote names under C05. Never use the agent app's release number as the model's identity. Never present a local hash as proof of the provider's hidden state.\n146\t\n147\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A fingerprint that covers only the recorded setup, with the provider's hidden details clearly outside the claim. Track changes behind remote names under C05. Missing hashes or model references for artifacts you control still fail this G1 item.\n148\t\n149\t### Verification recipe\n150\t\n151\tHash the same frozen manifest twice. The IDs must match. Change the container, harness, prompt, model/checkpoint/adapter reference, and configuration one at a time. Each change must produce a different ID. Confirm the exact input format for hashing is documented and that no secret values are exposed.\n152\t\n153\t**Evidence to keep:** Hashing steps and version, input manifests, expected and actual IDs.\n154\t\n155\t### Checks for likely_pass\n156\t\n157\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n158\t\n159\t- **A03.1** The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n160\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n161\t- **A03.2** The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n162\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n163\t- **A03.3** The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n164\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n165\t- **A03.4** The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID. *(A document can show this.)*\n166\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n167\t- **A03.5** Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n168\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n169\t\n170\t### Not covered here\n171\t\n172\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n173\t\n174\t- Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID → **C03**\n175\t- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n176\t- Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records → **C01**\n177\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n178\t\n179\t### Heuristics\n180\t\n181\t**Provable from code:** partly\n182\t\n183\t**Look for:**\n184\t- A manifest listing the hash inputs: container digest, harness version or files, system prompt, model ID and version, checkpoint/adapter references, and configuration.\n185\t- Code or a CI step that computes `agent.type.id` as a hash (for example `sha256:`) over that manifest with documented, canonical serialization (sorted keys, fixed encoding).\n186\t- Container images referenced by digest (`image@sha256:...`), not only by tag, in the manifest.\n187\t- A test that the same manifest gives the same ID and that changing each input changes it.\n188\t- Requested model ID and provider-reported served version recorded as separate fields.\n189\t\n190\t**Red flags:**\n191\t- `agent.type.id` is a hand-picked label, app version string, or git tag, not a content hash.\n192\t- The hash covers only some inputs (for example code but not the system prompt or model ID).\n193\t- Image referenced as `:latest` or a mutable tag, so the container input is not fixed.\n194\t- Secret values (API keys, tokens) are fed into the hash input or written into the manifest.\n195\t- The app release number is used as the model's identity.\n196\t\n197\t**Where to look:**\n198\t- .brace/manifest.yaml or other release manifest files\n199\t- CI pipelines and build scripts that compute or stamp the type ID\n200\t- Dockerfile, k8s Deployment image fields, Helm values\n201\t- prompt files and model config (where model ID is set)\n202\t\n203\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show the hashing method; proving it is stable and sensitive to each input needs the A03 recipe (hash a frozen manifest twice, then change each input one at a time). A hash can't prove a hosted model's hidden weights stayed the same; that belongs to C05.\n204\t\n205\t**N/A when:** never\n206\t\n207\t## A04 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Trustworthy attribution\n208\t\n209\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n210\t\n211\t### Requirement\n212\t\n213\tAssign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n214\t\n215\t### Verification recipe\n216\t\n217\tSend fake tenant or instance labels through arguments or headers the agent controls. The trusted layer must reject or replace them using verified identity. Follow an async job and confirm it keeps the right attribution. Run an action on behalf of a user or system, and confirm the record names that delegated subject separately from the agent.\n218\t\n219\t**Evidence to keep:** Fake-input fixtures, trusted identity logs, async trace.\n220\t\n221\t### Checks for likely_pass\n222\t\n223\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n224\t\n225\t- **A04.1** Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n226\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n227\t- **A04.2** Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n228\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n229\t- **A04.3** The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n230\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n231\t- **A04.4** When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n232\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n233\t- **A04.5** Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n234\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n235\t\n236\t### Not covered here\n237\t\n238\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n239\t\n240\t- Agent sharing the launching user's identity → **A01**\n241\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n242\t- Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority → **E08**\n243\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n244\t\n245\t### Heuristics\n246\t\n247\t**Provable from code:** yes\n248\t\n249\t**Look for:**\n250\t- Tenant ID and instance ID are taken from verified identity in the harness or gateway (token claims, session bound at launch) and injected into tool calls server-side.\n251\t- Tool handlers ignore or overwrite any tenant, user, or instance field in model-produced arguments.\n252\t- The delegated subject (user or system) is recorded in its own field, separate from the agent principal.\n253\t- Async job payloads carry trace context and verified identity, and workers re-check identity rather than trusting the payload.\n254\t- Authorization checks use verified principals, never trace IDs or request labels.\n255\t\n256\t**Red flags:**\n257\t- Tenant ID taken from a model-produced tool argument (for example `tenant_id` in the tool JSON schema the model fills).\n258\t- Instance or tenant labels read from caller-controlled HTTP headers (for example `X-Tenant-Id`) without checking them against the token.\n259\t- Trace IDs, span IDs, or correlation IDs used for authorization or to look up permissions.\n260\t- Audit records store only the agent identity, or only the user identity, with no separate delegated subject.\n261\t- Queued jobs lose trace context, or workers re-derive tenant from job data the agent wrote.\n262\t\n263\t**Where to look:**\n264\t- tool JSON schemas and function signatures exposed to the model\n265\t- tool handler code and gateway/middleware auth\n266\t- queue producer/consumer code and job payload schemas\n267\t- audit record schema\n268\t\n269\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code review can usually find the gap; the A04 recipe (fake tenant labels in arguments and headers, async attribution trace) confirms the trusted layer rejects or replaces them at runtime.\n270\t\n271\t**N/A when:** never\n272\t\n273\t## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n274\t\n275\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n276\t\n277\t### Requirement\n278\t\n279\tStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n280\t\n281\t### Verification recipe\n282\t\n283\tGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n284\t\n285\t**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n286\t\n287\t### Checks for likely_pass\n288\t\n289\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n290\t\n291\t- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n292\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n293\t- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n294\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n295\t- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n296\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n297\t\n298\t### Not covered here\n299\t\n300\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n301\t\n302\t- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n303\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n304\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n305\t\n306\t### Heuristics\n307\t\n308\t**Provable from code:** partly\n309\t\n310\t**Look for:**\n311\t- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n312\t- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n313\t- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n314\t- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n315\t\n316\t**Red flags:**\n317\t- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n318\t- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n319\t- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n320\t- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n321\t\n322\t**Where to look:**\n323\t- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n324\t- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n325\t- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n326\t- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n327\t\n328\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n329\t\n330\t**N/A when:** never\n331\t\n332\t## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n333\t\n334\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n335\t\n336\t### Requirement\n337\t\n338\tFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n339\t\n340\t### Verification recipe\n341\t\n342\tStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n343\t\n344\t**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n345\t\n346\t### Checks for likely_pass\n347\t\n348\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n349\t\n350\t- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n351\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n352\t- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n353\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n354\t- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n355\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n356\t- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n357\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n358\t\n359\t### Not covered here\n360\t\n361\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n362\t\n363\t- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n364\t- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n365\t- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n366\t\n367\t### Heuristics\n368\t\n369\t**Provable from code:** partly\n370\t\n371\t**Look for:**\n372\t- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n373\t- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n374\t- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n375\t- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n376\t- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n377\t\n378\t**Red flags:**\n379\t- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n380\t- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n381\t- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n382\t- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n383\t- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n384\t\n385\t**Where to look:**\n386\t- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n387\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n388\t- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n389\t\n390\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n391\t\n392\t**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n393\t\n394\t## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n395\t\n396\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n397\t\n398\t### Requirement\n399\t\n400\tList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n401\t\n402\t### Verification recipe\n403\t\n404\tList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n405\t\n406\t**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n407\t\n408\t### Checks for likely_pass\n409\t\n410\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n411\t\n412\t- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n413\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n414\t- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n415\t  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n416\t- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n417\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n418\t- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n419\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n420\t\n421\t### Not covered here\n422\t\n423\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n424\t\n425\t- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n426\t- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n427\t- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n428\t\n429\t### Heuristics\n430\t\n431\t**Provable from code:** partly\n432\t\n433\t**Look for:**\n434\t- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n435\t- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n436\t- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n437\t- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n438\t\n439\t**Red flags:**\n440\t- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n441\t- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n442\t- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n443\t- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n444\t- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n445\t\n446\t**Where to look:**\n447\t- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n448\t- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n449\t- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n450\t- model routing and fallback config\n451\t\n452\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n453\t\n454\t**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n455\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"e5a80a8a-d176-4ff9-9282-cfcb2525618f","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:39.430Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/agent.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# A — Agent\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## A01 · G1 · C2/Obs-T1 — Distinct agent identity\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nGive the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nLaunch the agent from a user account. Look at the identity (principal) the agent was issued, and compare the user's permissions with the agent's. Revoke only the agent and try again. Its identity and access must be separate from the user's and from unrelated agents.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Identity bindings, permission comparison, revocation results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A01.1** The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n- **A01.2** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n- **A01.3** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n- **A01.4** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed → **B02**\n- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- The agent runs as its own workload identity: a dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccount, cloud IAM role (AWS IAM role, GCP service account, Azure managed identity), SPIFFE ID, or Entra Agent ID, not shared with other services.\n- Tool and API clients get credentials from that identity (workload identity federation, IRSA, token exchange per RFC 8693) instead of from the user session.\n- When a user starts a run, the user is passed as a delegated subject (for example an `act`/`sub` pair in an exchanged token), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n- Permissions are attached to the agent principal in IaC (Terraform IAM policies, RBAC RoleBindings) and are narrower than the launching user's.\n- A documented way to revoke or disable the agent principal alone (delete role binding, disable service principal, rotate its key).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Agent calls downstream APIs with the launching user's OAuth access token or session cookie, forwarded as-is.\n- One service account, API key, or IAM role is shared by several unrelated agents or by the agent and the main app backend.\n- Long-lived static API keys for the agent in env files or code, with no per-agent principal behind them.\n- Agent identity is only a string label in config or a prompt (for example `agent_name: support-bot`) with no credential bound to it.\n- Revoking access would require disabling the user or a shared credential, because there is no agent-only principal.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Kubernetes ServiceAccount, RoleBinding, and pod spec `serviceAccountName`\n- Terraform/CloudFormation/Bicep IAM roles, policies, and identity federation\n- auth and credential setup in the harness and tool clients (token exchange, OAuth client config)\n- .env files, secret manager references, and Helm values\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show how identity is wired, not that the issued principal and its live permissions differ from the user's, or that revoking only the agent works. Needs the A01 recipe: launch from a user account, compare permissions, revoke the agent alone, and retry.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A02 · G1 · Obs-T1 — All six identity fields\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Business owner, Governance.\n\n### Requirement\n\nCheck that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nQuery every record from a controlled run with successful, denied, failed, async, and background actions. Count missing or empty values for each of the six fields. The count must be zero. Confirm the values match the known run and tenant. Confirm tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage or tracestate headers, which have no integrity protection.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Field-completeness query, counts, sample records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A02.1** The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the audit record schema or the function that writes audit entries. 2) Check for each of the six: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, trace context (`traceparent` or trace and span IDs). 3) Answer no if any one is missing from the schema or never set.\n- **A02.2** All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that writes an audit record, or should: success, policy deny, `except`/`catch` blocks, retries, queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks, other endpoints such as approvals, and refusals before the run starts (quota, rate limit, auth) that return an error without logging. 2) For each, check all six fields are set, ideally by one shared wrapper. 3) Answer no if any deny, error, async, or background path writes records without them, or writes no record at all.\n- **A02.3** Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check it stores trace and span IDs (or `traceparent`) as fields in the record itself. 3) Answer no if trace context exists only in OpenTelemetry spans, or the record's trace field is never filled.\n- **A02.4** Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tenant and owner values for audit records are read. 2) Trace each back to its source: token claims, verified session, IAM tags, or SPIFFE path, versus OTel baggage, `tracestate`, headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, request body, or tool arguments. 3) Answer no if any path takes them from an unprotected source.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Identity labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Sub-agent record missing its parent link or parent prompt → **A06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Telemetry or audit code sets all six fields on every action: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, `agent.type.id`, `agent.instance.id`, and W3C trace context (`traceparent`).\n- Fields are set in one shared place (span processor, logging middleware, audit wrapper around tool dispatch) so every tool call inherits them.\n- Denied, failed, retried, async, and background paths (queue workers, cron jobs, webhooks) also emit the fields, not just the main request handler.\n- Tenant and owner values come from verified identity (token claims, IAM tags, SPIFFE path) in the trusted layer.\n- A test or query that counts missing or empty identity fields per record.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Identity attributes are added only on the success path; `except`/`catch` blocks and policy-denial branches log without them.\n- Background jobs or queue consumers start new traces with no tenant, instance, or type ID.\n- Tenant or owner is read from OTel baggage or `tracestate`, which have no integrity protection.\n- `agent.type.id` or `agent.instance.id` never appears anywhere in the code or config.\n- `gen_ai.agent.id` is overwritten with a per-run ID and used as the only agent identifier.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry setup: span processors, resource attributes, instrumentation config\n- tool dispatch and policy-check code, including error and deny branches\n- queue workers, schedulers, webhook handlers\n- audit log writer and log schema definitions\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't prove every emitted record is complete or that values match the real run and tenant. Needs the A02 recipe: query all records from a controlled run with success, deny, failure, async, and background actions and show zero missing values.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Content-derived type identity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nCompute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n\n  Why this is hard: A hash only covers the inputs you can see. It can't prove that a hosted model's hidden weights or training stayed the same behind the same name.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Hash the exact local artifacts and the model, checkpoint, and adapter references you have, using a manifest anyone can rebuild. Record the model name you asked for separately from the version the provider says it served. Mark hidden information clearly. State that the ID fingerprints this recorded setup. Handle changes behind remote names under C05. Never use the agent app's release number as the model's identity. Never present a local hash as proof of the provider's hidden state.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A fingerprint that covers only the recorded setup, with the provider's hidden details clearly outside the claim. Track changes behind remote names under C05. Missing hashes or model references for artifacts you control still fail this G1 item.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nHash the same frozen manifest twice. The IDs must match. Change the container, harness, prompt, model/checkpoint/adapter reference, and configuration one at a time. Each change must produce a different ID. Confirm the exact input format for hashing is documented and that no secret values are exposed.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Hashing steps and version, input manifests, expected and actual IDs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A03.1** The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code or CI step that computes `agent.type.id` (search `sha256`, `hashlib`, `createHash`, `type_id`). 2) List its inputs. 3) Check each is there: container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, configuration. 4) Answer no if the ID is a label, version string, or git tag, or any input is missing.\n- **A03.2** The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the function that lists files to hash. 2) Write down every filter it applies: hidden files, names starting with '_' or '__', extensions, ignore lists, glob patterns. 3) For each filter, check whether a file that defines tools, prompts, or config would be skipped (for example `__init__.py`, `index.ts`, `.env`, `config/*.local.*`). 4) Answer no if any such file is skipped.\n- **A03.3** The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the model ID that goes into the manifest or hash. 2) Follow env vars and config to the final value, including defaults such as `getenv('MODEL', ...)`. 3) Answer no if it is an alias (`-latest`, no date or version), falls back to one when unset, or is the app release number. Also no if adapters or checkpoints are used but left out.\n- **A03.4** The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the serialization step before hashing. 2) Check for a fixed order: sorted keys (`sort_keys=True`), sorted file lists, fixed encoding and line endings. 3) Check file lists are not taken straight from directory listing or map iteration order. 4) Answer no if any unsorted dict, map, or filesystem order reaches the hash.\n- **A03.5** Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Changed running config still reporting the old approved ID → **C03**\n- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n- Release manifest missing training job, dataset, or model provider records → **C01**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A manifest listing the hash inputs: container digest, harness version or files, system prompt, model ID and version, checkpoint/adapter references, and configuration.\n- Code or a CI step that computes `agent.type.id` as a hash (for example `sha256:`) over that manifest with documented, canonical serialization (sorted keys, fixed encoding).\n- Container images referenced by digest (`image@sha256:...`), not only by tag, in the manifest.\n- A test that the same manifest gives the same ID and that changing each input changes it.\n- Requested model ID and provider-reported served version recorded as separate fields.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `agent.type.id` is a hand-picked label, app version string, or git tag, not a content hash.\n- The hash covers only some inputs (for example code but not the system prompt or model ID).\n- Image referenced as `:latest` or a mutable tag, so the container input is not fixed.\n- Secret values (API keys, tokens) are fed into the hash input or written into the manifest.\n- The app release number is used as the model's identity.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- .brace/manifest.yaml or other release manifest files\n- CI pipelines and build scripts that compute or stamp the type ID\n- Dockerfile, k8s Deployment image fields, Helm values\n- prompt files and model config (where model ID is set)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can show the hashing method; proving it is stable and sensitive to each input needs the A03 recipe (hash a frozen manifest twice, then change each input one at a time). A hash can't prove a hosted model's hidden weights stayed the same; that belongs to C05.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A04 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Trustworthy attribution\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAssign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend fake tenant or instance labels through arguments or headers the agent controls. The trusted layer must reject or replace them using verified identity. Follow an async job and confirm it keeps the right attribution. Run an action on behalf of a user or system, and confirm the record names that delegated subject separately from the agent.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fake-input fixtures, trusted identity logs, async trace.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A04.1** Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context.\n- **A04.2** Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues.\n- **A04.3** The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled.\n- **A04.4** When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data.\n- **A04.5** Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent sharing the launching user's identity → **A01**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n- Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority → **E08**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tenant ID and instance ID are taken from verified identity in the harness or gateway (token claims, session bound at launch) and injected into tool calls server-side.\n- Tool handlers ignore or overwrite any tenant, user, or instance field in model-produced arguments.\n- The delegated subject (user or system) is recorded in its own field, separate from the agent principal.\n- Async job payloads carry trace context and verified identity, and workers re-check identity rather than trusting the payload.\n- Authorization checks use verified principals, never trace IDs or request labels.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tenant ID taken from a model-produced tool argument (for example `tenant_id` in the tool JSON schema the model fills).\n- Instance or tenant labels read from caller-controlled HTTP headers (for example `X-Tenant-Id`) without checking them against the token.\n- Trace IDs, span IDs, or correlation IDs used for authorization or to look up permissions.\n- Audit records store only the agent identity, or only the user identity, with no separate delegated subject.\n- Queued jobs lose trace context, or workers re-derive tenant from job data the agent wrote.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- tool JSON schemas and function signatures exposed to the model\n- tool handler code and gateway/middleware auth\n- queue producer/consumer code and job payload schemas\n- audit record schema\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code review can usually find the gap; the A04 recipe (fake tenant labels in arguments and headers, async attribution trace) confirms the trusted layer rejects or replaces them at runtime.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n\n## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n- model routing and fallback config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n","numLines":455,"startLine":1,"totalLines":455}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfddpQHVT4EmChNY8eiYR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xw3bgG4fi7xoi35qAEmwbt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/configuration.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27176,"cache_read_input_tokens":118384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27176},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"bcba81ac-2c7f-4d5a-bd86-31f846066361","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:39.630Z","request_id":"req_011CfddpPyPFUaG6irgi5stv","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Xw3bgG4fi7xoi35qAEmwbt":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/configuration.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xw3bgG4fi7xoi35qAEmwbt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# C — Configuration\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## C01 · G2 · C1–C6/Obs-T1 — Complete release manifest\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Model team, Data and privacy.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tWrite a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.\n14\t\n15\t  Why this is hard: Teams can usually find their own training jobs and datasets. A hosted provider may not share its pretraining runs, weights, or data versions. Separate the records your team should have from the details the provider keeps private.\n16\t\n17\t  If you can't fully do it: Fill in model records from serving metadata, registries, and training-job output. Require full history for training your team runs. Clearly label fields the provider hides, and keep where and when you checked. Prefer a fixed provider snapshot when one exists. Record missing training history your team should have as a Gap, and rebuild it first. Never make up training versions. Never treat a provider alias as a training job ID.\n18\t\n19\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Provider pretraining details that are clearly marked as not shared, as long as your own model-selection and training records are complete. A G2 gap in visibility or history can get written acceptance with an owner. Unknown records must never be written down as real versions.\n20\t\n21\t### Verification recipe\n22\t\n23\tExport the deployed artifacts and settings, and match them field by field against the manifest. Fill in the provenance fields above for every model role. For each training job you ran, follow its output digest to the served checkpoint or adapter. Separate fields the provider doesn't share from records your team is missing. Check each requested model ID's snapshot-or-alias label against the cited provider doc.\n24\t\n25\t**Evidence to keep:** Manifest-vs-deployment diff, model registry records, training jobs, dataset versions.\n26\t\n27\t### Checks for likely_pass\n28\t\n29\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n30\t\n31\t- **C01.1** A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions. *(A document can show this.)*\n32\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.\n33\t- **C01.2** The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. *(A document can show this.)*\n34\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.\n35\t- **C01.3** Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.\n36\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.\n37\t- **C01.4** Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.\n38\t  - *How to verify:* 1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.\n39\t- **C01.5** For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n40\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo.\n41\t\n42\t### Not covered here\n43\t\n44\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n45\t\n46\t- Running setup not checked against the approved manifest → **C03**\n47\t- Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author → **C02**\n48\t- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n49\t- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n50\t\n51\t### Heuristics\n52\t\n53\t**Provable from code:** partly\n54\t\n55\t**Look for:**\n56\t- A release manifest file listing container digest, harness version, system prompt and rules files, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.\n57\t- A separate model record for each model role (main, sub-agent, judge, embedding, reranker) with provider, requested model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.\n58\t- Each requested model ID marked as pinned snapshot or changeable alias, with the provider doc and date cited.\n59\t- For self-run training: job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n60\t- Owners named per artifact, and immutable references (digests, commit SHAs) where they exist; optionally a CycloneDX ML-BOM or SPDX 3.0 AI profile.\n61\t\n62\t**Red flags:**\n63\t- Only an app version number or git tag identifies the release.\n64\t- Helper models (embedding, reranker, judge) are missing from the manifest.\n65\t- Provider alias written into a training job or version field, or made-up version values where the provider shares none.\n66\t- MCP servers, egress rules, or budgets are configured but not listed in the manifest.\n67\t- Model ID recorded with no pinned-or-alias label.\n68\t\n69\t**Where to look:**\n70\t- .brace/manifest.yaml, release manifests, SBOM/ML-BOM files (`*.cdx.json`, `bom.json`)\n71\t- model config files and env vars that set model IDs\n72\t- *.mcp.json, agent config, k8s NetworkPolicy, Helm values\n73\t- training pipeline configs and model registry references\n74\t\n75\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows what the manifest declares. Proving it matches the deployment needs the C01 recipe: export deployed artifacts and settings and diff them field by field, and follow training outputs to the served checkpoint.\n76\t\n77\t**N/A when:** never\n78\t\n79\t## C02 · G2 · C1–C7 — Review security-relevant changes\n80\t\n81\t**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n82\t\n83\t### Requirement\n84\t\n85\tRequire diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n86\t\n87\t### Verification recipe\n88\t\n89\tUse a role without release permission to try changing a prompt, a tool, and a network policy. Each change must be denied. Review one normal change and one controlled emergency change. Check for a named approver, an end date where needed, and a follow-up review.\n90\t\n91\t**Evidence to keep:** Role policy, rejected edits, normal and emergency change records.\n92\t\n93\t### Checks for likely_pass\n94\t\n95\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n96\t\n97\t- **C02.1** The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.\n98\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.\n99\t- **C02.2** Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.\n100\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.\n101\t- **C02.3** An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n102\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.\n103\t\n104\t### Not covered here\n105\t\n106\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n107\t\n108\t- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n109\t- Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases → **C06**\n110\t- No tested rollback to an approved setup → **C07**\n111\t\n112\t### Heuristics\n113\t\n114\t**Provable from code:** partly\n115\t\n116\t**Look for:**\n117\t- Branch protection and CODEOWNERS requiring review on prompts, tool configs, manifest, and policy files.\n118\t- Production config deploys only through CI/CD (GitOps, pipeline approvals), not manual edits.\n119\t- IAM or RBAC that limits who can change production config, prompts, and network policy.\n120\t- A documented emergency-change path that records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review.\n121\t\n122\t**Red flags:**\n123\t- Prompts or tool lists loaded from a database, admin UI, or feature-flag service that anyone with app access can edit without review.\n124\t- CODEOWNERS or required reviews missing for prompt, policy, or MCP config paths.\n125\t- Deploy scripts or `kubectl apply` run from laptops with broad production credentials.\n126\t- Break-glass access with no expiry or no follow-up record.\n127\t\n128\t**Where to look:**\n129\t- CODEOWNERS, branch protection settings, `.github/workflows` or other CI configs\n130\t- prompt storage (files vs database vs remote prompt registry)\n131\t- IAM/RBAC for deploy roles; GitOps config (Argo CD, Flux)\n132\t- change-management or break-glass docs\n133\t\n134\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C02 recipe: try changing a prompt, tool, and network policy with a role lacking release permission and see each denied, and review real normal and emergency change records.\n135\t\n136\t**N/A when:** never\n137\t\n138\t## C03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Match release identity to running state\n139\t\n140\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Operations, Model team.\n141\t\n142\t### Requirement\n143\t\n144\tFreeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.\n145\t\n146\t  Why this is hard: A manifest or an ID the agent reports about itself can stay the same while the running prompt, tool setup, or hosted model changes. You may not be able to inspect a provider's internals yourself.\n147\t\n148\t  If you can't fully do it: Observe local artifacts and deployment settings yourself, not from what the agent reports, and compare them with the manifest. Block or quarantine any mismatch you find. Check the served model's metadata when the provider shares it. Limit the claim to artifacts you control and provider references you can see. Track hidden remote state under C05. A provider's limits never excuse an unchecked local setup. Changed local artifacts must never keep their approved identity unnoticed.\n149\t\n150\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Checks limited to artifacts you control and provider references you can see, with hidden remote state handled under C05. More frequent checks may cost time and compute. Unchecked local artifacts, or unnoticed changes to declared local settings, still block release.\n151\t\n152\t### Verification recipe\n153\t\n154\tCompare the runtime artifact references you observe yourself with the approved manifest and the type ID the agent emits. In a test deployment, swap in a different prompt, model, self-hosted weights file, or tool setup. The mismatch must be caught or rejected. Don't trust the ID the agent reports about itself.\n155\t\n156\t**Evidence to keep:** Runtime export, manifest comparison, mismatch event.\n157\t\n158\t### Checks for likely_pass\n159\t\n160\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n161\t\n162\t- **C03.1** Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.\n163\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.\n164\t- **C03.2** A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports.\n165\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo.\n166\t- **C03.3** The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one.\n167\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID.\n168\t- **C03.4** Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy.\n169\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n170\t- **C03.5** A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it.\n171\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed.\n172\t\n173\t### Not covered here\n174\t\n175\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n176\t\n177\t- Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components → **C01**\n178\t- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n179\t- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n180\t- Container image not signature-checked at admission → **B10**\n181\t\n182\t### Heuristics\n183\t\n184\t**Provable from code:** partly\n185\t\n186\t**Look for:**\n187\t- A deploy-time or startup check that compares running artifacts (image digest, prompt hash, model ID, tool config, weights digest) with the approved manifest and fails on mismatch.\n188\t- The emitted `agent.type.id` is computed from the artifacts actually loaded, not read from a static config value.\n189\t- Admission control that verifies image signatures and digests (for example Sigstore policy-controller, Kyverno, Gatekeeper).\n190\t- Self-hosted weights and adapters checked against digest and signature before load.\n191\t\n192\t**Red flags:**\n193\t- `agent.type.id` is a constant in config or env that stays the same when the prompt or model changes.\n194\t- Prompts or tool definitions fetched at runtime from a mutable source with no hash check.\n195\t- Images deployed by mutable tag with no digest verification.\n196\t- The only check is the agent reporting its own ID.\n197\t\n198\t**Where to look:**\n199\t- startup and bootstrap code in the harness\n200\t- CI/CD deploy steps and admission policies\n201\t- model loading code for self-hosted weights\n202\t- prompt and tool config loaders\n203\t\n204\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C03 recipe: observe runtime artifact references independently, compare to the manifest and emitted ID, and swap a prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup in a test deployment to show the mismatch is caught.\n205\t\n206\t**N/A when:** never\n207\t\n208\t## C04 · G2 · C1/C3/C4/Obs-T1 — Detect drift per agent\n209\t\n210\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security, Model team.\n211\t\n212\t### Requirement\n213\t\n214\tCompare running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against their approved baseline. Simulate a prompt edit, an added tool, and a looser egress rule. Check that each one raises an alert and gets the documented response — block, quarantine, or rollback — within a set time.\n215\t\n216\t### Verification recipe\n217\t\n218\tIn a separate deployment, change the prompt, the MCP server list, and the egress policy one at a time, outside the approved baseline. Measure how long detection and response take. Every change must trigger the documented action within the agreed time.\n219\t\n220\t**Evidence to keep:** State before and after, drift alerts, quarantine or rollback events.\n221\t\n222\t### Checks for likely_pass\n223\t\n224\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n225\t\n226\t- **C04.1** A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered.\n227\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform.\n228\t- **C04.2** The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events.\n229\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo.\n230\t- **C04.3** Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit.\n231\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift.\n232\t\n233\t### Not covered here\n234\t\n235\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n236\t\n237\t- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n238\t- A tool server changing its description or schema after approval → **E03**\n239\t- Who may change production config, and emergency change records → **C02**\n240\t- Manifest missing components that define the release → **C01**\n241\t\n242\t### Heuristics\n243\t\n244\t**Provable from code:** partly\n245\t\n246\t**Look for:**\n247\t- A drift detector that compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted weights against a baseline per agent.\n248\t- GitOps self-heal or drift alerts (for example Argo CD out-of-sync alerts, Terraform drift checks, cloud config rules).\n249\t- Alert rules that route drift to a named response: block, quarantine, or rollback, with a time target.\n250\t- Runtime hashing of prompts and MCP config compared with the manifest on a schedule.\n251\t\n252\t**Red flags:**\n253\t- Drift checks cover infrastructure only, not prompts, MCP server lists, or tool config.\n254\t- Drift alerts exist but have no owner, no response action, or no time target.\n255\t- MCP servers can be added at runtime (user-editable config, dynamic discovery) with no baseline comparison.\n256\t- Egress policy managed outside version control.\n257\t\n258\t**Where to look:**\n259\t- GitOps and IaC drift settings\n260\t- monitoring and alert rules\n261\t- *.mcp.json and runtime config reload code\n262\t- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy config\n263\t\n264\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C04 drill: change the prompt, MCP server list, and egress policy one at a time outside the baseline and measure detection and response time.\n265\t\n266\t**N/A when:** never\n267\t\n268\t## C05 · G2 · C3/C4/C7 — Control dependency, model, and training changes\n269\t\n270\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security, Vendors.\n271\t\n272\t### Requirement\n273\t\n274\tPin dependency versions. Where supported, also pin each model's base version, checkpoint or weights, and fine-tune or adapter version. Before promotion, link any changed training run, dataset, or training code or config to the model it produced and to its test results. Record the model version actually served, when the runtime or provider shares it, separately from the name you requested. Before loading weights, adapters, or datasets you host, check their signature against an approved signer (for example, with OpenSSF Model Signing). Also check their file format. Pickle-based formats can run code when loaded, so they need an approved exception and a scan. Test swapping a model, checkpoint, or adapter, and check that it triggers review and re-testing. Some remote services or names can change behind a fixed name. For those, record missing training and version details clearly. Watch available version metadata and provider notices, and decide when to re-test or stop using the model. A local config hash alone can't prove remote behavior is unchanged. A pinned snapshot fixes the weights, not the provider's serving systems, which can change under the same ID. Keep scheduled re-testing.\n275\t\n276\t  Why this is hard: A hosted service may change behind a name without sharing a version ID or training history. Behavior tests can catch some changes, but they can't prove nothing hidden changed.\n277\t\n278\t  If you can't fully do it: Prefer pinned snapshots. Otherwise keep both the requested and the observed IDs, watch provider notices, and schedule realistic tests with a named owner. Limit permissions and autonomy while you're unsure of the version. Decide in advance what triggers a pause or a move to an approved alternative. Record gaps from names that can change, and any allowed risk acceptance. Tests and provider notices help manage uncertainty. They don't prove the model's identity is fixed.\n279\t\n280\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: The ease of a hosted model, with less reproducibility and possibly slower detection of changes, once that exposure has written G2 acceptance. Set a testing schedule and decide when use must stop. If the uncertainty matters too much, choose a pinned alternative or reduce the agent's powers.\n281\t\n282\t### Verification recipe\n283\t\n284\tReplace a checkpoint or adapter, and confirm promotion requires review and testing. Load a weights folder with one changed file and one added file. Also load an unsigned or pickle-based artifact that has no approved exception. Loading must be refused. For training you run, trace a changed dataset, code, or config through a new run to its output. For hosted aliases, test a change in served-version metadata when the provider shares it. Otherwise, run the documented process for provider notices or scheduled re-testing, and record what it can't detect.\n285\t\n286\t**Evidence to keep:** Version diffs, training-to-output history, promotion block, provider monitoring record.\n287\t\n288\t### Checks for likely_pass\n289\t\n290\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n291\t\n292\t- **C05.1** Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags.\n293\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile.\n294\t- **C05.2** Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'.\n295\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it.\n296\t- **C05.3** Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails.\n297\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n298\t- **C05.4** Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded.\n299\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check.\n300\t- **C05.5** The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name.\n301\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value.\n302\t\n303\t### Not covered here\n304\t\n305\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n306\t\n307\t- Tool or MCP server approved without reviewing publisher, permissions, or vulnerabilities → **E02**\n308\t- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n309\t- Rollback setup naming a hosted model that is retired → **C07**\n310\t\n311\t### Heuristics\n312\t\n313\t**Provable from code:** partly\n314\t\n315\t**Look for:**\n316\t- Lockfiles with exact versions or hashes for dependencies (`package-lock.json`, `poetry.lock`, `uv.lock`, `go.sum`).\n317\t- Model IDs pinned to dated or snapshot versions where the provider offers them; requested and served model versions both logged.\n318\t- Weights, adapters, and datasets you host are signature-checked against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing) before load.\n319\t- Safe formats (for example safetensors) required; pickle-based loads blocked unless there is an approved exception and scan.\n320\t- Promotion gates that link a changed training run, dataset, or training config to the produced model and its test results; scheduled re-tests for aliases.\n321\t\n322\t**Red flags:**\n323\t- Model alias like `-latest` or an unversioned family name in production config.\n324\t- `torch.load`, `pickle.load`, or `trust_remote_code=True` on weights with no signature check or exception record.\n325\t- Dependencies with ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`) and no lockfile.\n326\t- Weights or adapters downloaded at startup from a hub by name with no revision or digest pin.\n327\t- Response metadata with the served model version is discarded.\n328\t\n329\t**Where to look:**\n330\t- lockfiles and dependency manifests\n331\t- model config and env vars for model IDs\n332\t- model and adapter loading code\n333\t- training pipelines and promotion workflows\n334\t\n335\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows pinning and load checks. The C05 recipe proves a swapped checkpoint triggers review, changed or unsigned weights are refused, and the provider notice or re-test process runs. Hidden provider changes behind a fixed ID can't be proven from code.\n336\t\n337\t**N/A when:** never\n338\t\n339\t## C06 · G2 · C4–C9 — Re-test changed behavior\n340\t\n341\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Business owner.\n342\t\n343\t### Requirement\n344\t\n345\tBefore promotion, run typical allowed tasks and attack cases against the release candidate. Include destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping. Include retrieval, memory, and checker tests where they apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Keep the results for the exact candidate manifest.\n346\t\n347\t### Verification recipe\n348\t\n349\tRun the release test suite for the exact candidate and model artifacts. Include allowed tasks and the attack cases from this guide that apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Compare results to pass criteria set in advance. Block promotion on any blocking failure.\n350\t\n351\t**Evidence to keep:** Suite version, candidate manifest and model IDs, results, release-gate decision.\n352\t\n353\t### Checks for likely_pass\n354\t\n355\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n356\t\n357\t- **C06.1** A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure.\n358\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it.\n359\t- **C06.2** The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply.\n360\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers).\n361\t- **C06.3** Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest.\n362\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored.\n363\t\n364\t### Not covered here\n365\t\n366\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n367\t\n368\t- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n369\t- No tested rollback to an approved setup → **C07**\n370\t- Config changes made without a reviewed diff and named author → **C02**\n371\t\n372\t### Heuristics\n373\t\n374\t**Provable from code:** partly\n375\t\n376\t**Look for:**\n377\t- A release test suite in CI that runs allowed tasks plus attack cases: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping.\n378\t- Retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist; monitoring-evasion and stop-path tests if misalignment is in scope.\n379\t- Pass criteria defined in advance in config or test code.\n380\t- The release pipeline blocks promotion on any blocking failure and stores results keyed to the candidate manifest and model IDs.\n381\t\n382\t**Red flags:**\n383\t- Eval jobs marked `continue-on-error` or allowed to fail without blocking deploy.\n384\t- Only happy-path or quality evals; no attack or denial cases.\n385\t- Tests run against a mocked model or a different model ID than the release candidate.\n386\t- Results not stored or not tied to a specific manifest.\n387\t\n388\t**Where to look:**\n389\t- CI workflows and release pipelines\n390\t- eval and test directories (red-team suites, promptfoo, inspect, pytest suites)\n391\t- release gate scripts\n392\t\n393\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C06 run: suite version, candidate manifest and model IDs, results, and the gate decision for the exact release candidate.\n394\t\n395\t**N/A when:** never\n396\t\n397\t## C07 · G2 · C8/C9 — Rollback and restart\n398\t\n399\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n400\t\n401\t### Requirement\n402\t\n403\tKeep an approved recovery setup and show that rollback works. Confirm each hosted model it names is still offered, and record the announced retirement date. Account for changed credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work. Don't restore revoked permissions or replay unsafe side effects just because the old artifact still exists.\n404\t\n405\t### Verification recipe\n406\t\n407\tUpgrade a test deployment, create pending work and a revoked credential, then roll back. Confirm the approved old artifact runs and each hosted model it names is still offered. Confirm the credential stays revoked. Confirm queues, memory, search state, and side effects match the recovery procedure.\n408\t\n409\t**Evidence to keep:** Rollback events, actual permissions, state and queue comparison.\n410\t\n411\t### Checks for likely_pass\n412\t\n413\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n414\t\n415\t- **C07.1** An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code.\n416\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists.\n417\t- **C07.2** Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access.\n418\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how.\n419\t- **C07.3** The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed.\n420\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is.\n421\t- **C07.4** Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date. *(A document can show this.)*\n422\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.\n423\t\n424\t### Not covered here\n425\t\n426\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n427\t\n428\t- Changed or tampered checkpoints accepted, or unsafe replay of actions → **R14**\n429\t- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n430\t- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n431\t\n432\t### Heuristics\n433\t\n434\t**Provable from code:** partly\n435\t\n436\t**Look for:**\n437\t- A documented rollback procedure and an approved previous release kept available (prior image digests, prior manifest).\n438\t- Rollback steps that handle credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work.\n439\t- Revocations stored outside the release artifact so a rollback does not restore them.\n440\t- Retirement dates recorded for each hosted model the recovery release names.\n441\t\n442\t**Red flags:**\n443\t- Rollback redeploys an old manifest that includes credentials or permissions since revoked.\n444\t- Queued jobs replay automatically after rollback with no check for already-done side effects.\n445\t- Recovery release names a hosted model with no check that it is still offered.\n446\t- No retained artifact for the previous release (images overwritten by tag).\n447\t\n448\t**Where to look:**\n449\t- runbooks and rollback scripts\n450\t- deploy pipeline rollback stages, Helm history, GitOps revisions\n451\t- queue and memory/index migration code\n452\t- secret and credential management config\n453\t\n454\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C07 drill: upgrade, create pending work and a revoked credential, roll back, and confirm the credential stays revoked and state and queues match the procedure.\n455\t\n456\t**N/A when:** never\n457\t\n458\t## C08 · G2 · Obs-T1/Obs-T2/Obs-T3 — Connect configuration to execution\n459\t\n460\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n461\t\n462\t### Requirement\n463\t\n464\tFrom a trace, look up the release manifest, the context sizes at each decision, and any prompts a parent passed to a sub-agent. Keep these links for the full audit retention period. Control access to sensitive artifacts.\n465\t\n466\t### Verification recipe\n467\t\n468\tPick a past trace. Using the incident-response role, pull its manifest, model records, context sizes, and any prompts passed from a parent. Confirm these links stay available for the required retention period under the storage policy.\n469\t\n470\t**Evidence to keep:** Lookup results, retention and access settings, missing-link check.\n471\t\n472\t### Checks for likely_pass\n473\t\n474\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n475\t\n476\t- **C08.1** Each trace or audit record carries the agent-type-id or manifest reference that resolves to the stored release manifest.\n477\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the trace or audit record writer. 2) Check every record carries `agent.type.id` or a manifest reference. 3) Check that value is the key of a stored manifest. 4) Answer no if the field is missing on some paths (errors, background jobs) or holds a value that resolves to no stored manifest.\n478\t- **C08.2** The context size at each model decision is recorded in the trace.\n479\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find model call sites and their spans. 2) Look for the context size recorded per call (for example `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` or a context-length attribute). 3) Answer no if it is missing on any model call path, including retries and sub-agents.\n480\t- **C08.3** Prompts passed from a parent to a sub-agent are stored and linked from the trace.\n481\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code. 2) Check the parent's prompt is stored and the trace holds a link to it (ID, hash, or URI). 3) Answer no if the prompt is not stored, prompt capture is off, or the trace has no link.\n482\t- **C08.4** Storage config keeps manifests, traces, and these links for at least the audit retention period, and limits access to sensitive artifacts by role.\n483\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find storage config for manifests, traces, and prompt links: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention, TTLs. 2) Check retention covers the audit period for all of them. 3) Check IAM limits sensitive artifacts by role. 4) Answer no if any has a shorter TTL, no retention, or broad read access. Answer unknown if storage is configured outside this repo.\n484\t\n485\t### Not covered here\n486\t\n487\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n488\t\n489\t- Context size not measured or logged for each action decision → **R07**\n490\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n491\t- Manifest missing components that define the release → **C01**\n492\t\n493\t### Heuristics\n494\t\n495\t**Provable from code:** partly\n496\t\n497\t**Look for:**\n498\t- Traces carry `agent.type.id` linking to a stored manifest, `agent.context.size` per decision, and `agent.parent.prompt` (or its hash reference) for sub-agents.\n499\t- Manifest archive keyed by type ID with retention at least as long as the audit retention period.\n500\t- Storage policies (bucket lifecycle, log retention settings) that match the required retention.\n501\t- Access controls on sensitive artifacts such as prompts and parent prompts.\n502\t\n503\t**Red flags:**\n504\t- `agent.context.size` missing, or emitted as zero when unknown.\n505\t- Manifests or prompts kept for less time than traces, so old traces can't be resolved.\n506\t- Trace retention set to a short default (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer audit export.\n507\t- Sensitive prompt artifacts readable by everyone with log access.\n508\t\n509\t**Where to look:**\n510\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and exporter config\n511\t- log and trace backend retention settings, bucket lifecycle rules\n512\t- manifest storage and artifact registry config\n513\t\n514\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C08 recipe: from a past trace, pull the manifest, model records, context sizes, and parent prompts with the incident-response role, and confirm retention settings.\n515\t\n516\t**N/A when:** never\n517\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"5146ba00-e7d0-4903-95a7-ffd93ac49557","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:39.935Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/configuration.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# C — Configuration\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## C01 · G2 · C1–C6/Obs-T1 — Complete release manifest\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Model team, Data and privacy.\n\n### Requirement\n\nWrite a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.\n\n  Why this is hard: Teams can usually find their own training jobs and datasets. A hosted provider may not share its pretraining runs, weights, or data versions. Separate the records your team should have from the details the provider keeps private.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Fill in model records from serving metadata, registries, and training-job output. Require full history for training your team runs. Clearly label fields the provider hides, and keep where and when you checked. Prefer a fixed provider snapshot when one exists. Record missing training history your team should have as a Gap, and rebuild it first. Never make up training versions. Never treat a provider alias as a training job ID.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Provider pretraining details that are clearly marked as not shared, as long as your own model-selection and training records are complete. A G2 gap in visibility or history can get written acceptance with an owner. Unknown records must never be written down as real versions.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nExport the deployed artifacts and settings, and match them field by field against the manifest. Fill in the provenance fields above for every model role. For each training job you ran, follow its output digest to the served checkpoint or adapter. Separate fields the provider doesn't share from records your team is missing. Check each requested model ID's snapshot-or-alias label against the cited provider doc.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Manifest-vs-deployment diff, model registry records, training jobs, dataset versions.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C01.1** A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.\n- **C01.2** The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.\n- **C01.3** Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.\n- **C01.4** Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.\n- **C01.5** For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Running setup not checked against the approved manifest → **C03**\n- Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author → **C02**\n- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A release manifest file listing container digest, harness version, system prompt and rules files, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.\n- A separate model record for each model role (main, sub-agent, judge, embedding, reranker) with provider, requested model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.\n- Each requested model ID marked as pinned snapshot or changeable alias, with the provider doc and date cited.\n- For self-run training: job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.\n- Owners named per artifact, and immutable references (digests, commit SHAs) where they exist; optionally a CycloneDX ML-BOM or SPDX 3.0 AI profile.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Only an app version number or git tag identifies the release.\n- Helper models (embedding, reranker, judge) are missing from the manifest.\n- Provider alias written into a training job or version field, or made-up version values where the provider shares none.\n- MCP servers, egress rules, or budgets are configured but not listed in the manifest.\n- Model ID recorded with no pinned-or-alias label.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- .brace/manifest.yaml, release manifests, SBOM/ML-BOM files (`*.cdx.json`, `bom.json`)\n- model config files and env vars that set model IDs\n- *.mcp.json, agent config, k8s NetworkPolicy, Helm values\n- training pipeline configs and model registry references\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows what the manifest declares. Proving it matches the deployment needs the C01 recipe: export deployed artifacts and settings and diff them field by field, and follow training outputs to the served checkpoint.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C02 · G2 · C1–C7 — Review security-relevant changes\n\n**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRequire diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nUse a role without release permission to try changing a prompt, a tool, and a network policy. Each change must be denied. Review one normal change and one controlled emergency change. Check for a named approver, an end date where needed, and a follow-up review.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Role policy, rejected edits, normal and emergency change records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C02.1** The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.\n- **C02.2** Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.\n- **C02.3** An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n- Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases → **C06**\n- No tested rollback to an approved setup → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Branch protection and CODEOWNERS requiring review on prompts, tool configs, manifest, and policy files.\n- Production config deploys only through CI/CD (GitOps, pipeline approvals), not manual edits.\n- IAM or RBAC that limits who can change production config, prompts, and network policy.\n- A documented emergency-change path that records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Prompts or tool lists loaded from a database, admin UI, or feature-flag service that anyone with app access can edit without review.\n- CODEOWNERS or required reviews missing for prompt, policy, or MCP config paths.\n- Deploy scripts or `kubectl apply` run from laptops with broad production credentials.\n- Break-glass access with no expiry or no follow-up record.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- CODEOWNERS, branch protection settings, `.github/workflows` or other CI configs\n- prompt storage (files vs database vs remote prompt registry)\n- IAM/RBAC for deploy roles; GitOps config (Argo CD, Flux)\n- change-management or break-glass docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C02 recipe: try changing a prompt, tool, and network policy with a role lacking release permission and see each denied, and review real normal and emergency change records.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C03 · G1 · Obs-T1 — Match release identity to running state\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Operations, Model team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFreeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.\n\n  Why this is hard: A manifest or an ID the agent reports about itself can stay the same while the running prompt, tool setup, or hosted model changes. You may not be able to inspect a provider's internals yourself.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Observe local artifacts and deployment settings yourself, not from what the agent reports, and compare them with the manifest. Block or quarantine any mismatch you find. Check the served model's metadata when the provider shares it. Limit the claim to artifacts you control and provider references you can see. Track hidden remote state under C05. A provider's limits never excuse an unchecked local setup. Changed local artifacts must never keep their approved identity unnoticed.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Checks limited to artifacts you control and provider references you can see, with hidden remote state handled under C05. More frequent checks may cost time and compute. Unchecked local artifacts, or unnoticed changes to declared local settings, still block release.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCompare the runtime artifact references you observe yourself with the approved manifest and the type ID the agent emits. In a test deployment, swap in a different prompt, model, self-hosted weights file, or tool setup. The mismatch must be caught or rejected. Don't trust the ID the agent reports about itself.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Runtime export, manifest comparison, mismatch event.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C03.1** Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.\n- **C03.2** A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo.\n- **C03.3** The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID.\n- **C03.4** Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n- **C03.5** A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components → **C01**\n- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n- Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs → **A03**\n- Container image not signature-checked at admission → **B10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A deploy-time or startup check that compares running artifacts (image digest, prompt hash, model ID, tool config, weights digest) with the approved manifest and fails on mismatch.\n- The emitted `agent.type.id` is computed from the artifacts actually loaded, not read from a static config value.\n- Admission control that verifies image signatures and digests (for example Sigstore policy-controller, Kyverno, Gatekeeper).\n- Self-hosted weights and adapters checked against digest and signature before load.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `agent.type.id` is a constant in config or env that stays the same when the prompt or model changes.\n- Prompts or tool definitions fetched at runtime from a mutable source with no hash check.\n- Images deployed by mutable tag with no digest verification.\n- The only check is the agent reporting its own ID.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- startup and bootstrap code in the harness\n- CI/CD deploy steps and admission policies\n- model loading code for self-hosted weights\n- prompt and tool config loaders\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C03 recipe: observe runtime artifact references independently, compare to the manifest and emitted ID, and swap a prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup in a test deployment to show the mismatch is caught.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C04 · G2 · C1/C3/C4/Obs-T1 — Detect drift per agent\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security, Model team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nCompare running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against their approved baseline. Simulate a prompt edit, an added tool, and a looser egress rule. Check that each one raises an alert and gets the documented response — block, quarantine, or rollback — within a set time.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nIn a separate deployment, change the prompt, the MCP server list, and the egress policy one at a time, outside the approved baseline. Measure how long detection and response take. Every change must trigger the documented action within the agreed time.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** State before and after, drift alerts, quarantine or rollback events.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C04.1** A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform.\n- **C04.2** The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo.\n- **C04.3** Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n- A tool server changing its description or schema after approval → **E03**\n- Who may change production config, and emergency change records → **C02**\n- Manifest missing components that define the release → **C01**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A drift detector that compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted weights against a baseline per agent.\n- GitOps self-heal or drift alerts (for example Argo CD out-of-sync alerts, Terraform drift checks, cloud config rules).\n- Alert rules that route drift to a named response: block, quarantine, or rollback, with a time target.\n- Runtime hashing of prompts and MCP config compared with the manifest on a schedule.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Drift checks cover infrastructure only, not prompts, MCP server lists, or tool config.\n- Drift alerts exist but have no owner, no response action, or no time target.\n- MCP servers can be added at runtime (user-editable config, dynamic discovery) with no baseline comparison.\n- Egress policy managed outside version control.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- GitOps and IaC drift settings\n- monitoring and alert rules\n- *.mcp.json and runtime config reload code\n- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C04 drill: change the prompt, MCP server list, and egress policy one at a time outside the baseline and measure detection and response time.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C05 · G2 · C3/C4/C7 — Control dependency, model, and training changes\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nPin dependency versions. Where supported, also pin each model's base version, checkpoint or weights, and fine-tune or adapter version. Before promotion, link any changed training run, dataset, or training code or config to the model it produced and to its test results. Record the model version actually served, when the runtime or provider shares it, separately from the name you requested. Before loading weights, adapters, or datasets you host, check their signature against an approved signer (for example, with OpenSSF Model Signing). Also check their file format. Pickle-based formats can run code when loaded, so they need an approved exception and a scan. Test swapping a model, checkpoint, or adapter, and check that it triggers review and re-testing. Some remote services or names can change behind a fixed name. For those, record missing training and version details clearly. Watch available version metadata and provider notices, and decide when to re-test or stop using the model. A local config hash alone can't prove remote behavior is unchanged. A pinned snapshot fixes the weights, not the provider's serving systems, which can change under the same ID. Keep scheduled re-testing.\n\n  Why this is hard: A hosted service may change behind a name without sharing a version ID or training history. Behavior tests can catch some changes, but they can't prove nothing hidden changed.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Prefer pinned snapshots. Otherwise keep both the requested and the observed IDs, watch provider notices, and schedule realistic tests with a named owner. Limit permissions and autonomy while you're unsure of the version. Decide in advance what triggers a pause or a move to an approved alternative. Record gaps from names that can change, and any allowed risk acceptance. Tests and provider notices help manage uncertainty. They don't prove the model's identity is fixed.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: The ease of a hosted model, with less reproducibility and possibly slower detection of changes, once that exposure has written G2 acceptance. Set a testing schedule and decide when use must stop. If the uncertainty matters too much, choose a pinned alternative or reduce the agent's powers.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nReplace a checkpoint or adapter, and confirm promotion requires review and testing. Load a weights folder with one changed file and one added file. Also load an unsigned or pickle-based artifact that has no approved exception. Loading must be refused. For training you run, trace a changed dataset, code, or config through a new run to its output. For hosted aliases, test a change in served-version metadata when the provider shares it. Otherwise, run the documented process for provider notices or scheduled re-testing, and record what it can't detect.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Version diffs, training-to-output history, promotion block, provider monitoring record.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C05.1** Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile.\n- **C05.2** Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it.\n- **C05.3** Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n- **C05.4** Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check.\n- **C05.5** The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Tool or MCP server approved without reviewing publisher, permissions, or vulnerabilities → **E02**\n- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n- Rollback setup naming a hosted model that is retired → **C07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Lockfiles with exact versions or hashes for dependencies (`package-lock.json`, `poetry.lock`, `uv.lock`, `go.sum`).\n- Model IDs pinned to dated or snapshot versions where the provider offers them; requested and served model versions both logged.\n- Weights, adapters, and datasets you host are signature-checked against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing) before load.\n- Safe formats (for example safetensors) required; pickle-based loads blocked unless there is an approved exception and scan.\n- Promotion gates that link a changed training run, dataset, or training config to the produced model and its test results; scheduled re-tests for aliases.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Model alias like `-latest` or an unversioned family name in production config.\n- `torch.load`, `pickle.load`, or `trust_remote_code=True` on weights with no signature check or exception record.\n- Dependencies with ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`) and no lockfile.\n- Weights or adapters downloaded at startup from a hub by name with no revision or digest pin.\n- Response metadata with the served model version is discarded.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- lockfiles and dependency manifests\n- model config and env vars for model IDs\n- model and adapter loading code\n- training pipelines and promotion workflows\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows pinning and load checks. The C05 recipe proves a swapped checkpoint triggers review, changed or unsigned weights are refused, and the provider notice or re-test process runs. Hidden provider changes behind a fixed ID can't be proven from code.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C06 · G2 · C4–C9 — Re-test changed behavior\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nBefore promotion, run typical allowed tasks and attack cases against the release candidate. Include destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping. Include retrieval, memory, and checker tests where they apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Keep the results for the exact candidate manifest.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRun the release test suite for the exact candidate and model artifacts. Include allowed tasks and the attack cases from this guide that apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Compare results to pass criteria set in advance. Block promotion on any blocking failure.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Suite version, candidate manifest and model IDs, results, release-gate decision.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C06.1** A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it.\n- **C06.2** The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers).\n- **C06.3** Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed → **C04**\n- No tested rollback to an approved setup → **C07**\n- Config changes made without a reviewed diff and named author → **C02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A release test suite in CI that runs allowed tasks plus attack cases: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping.\n- Retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist; monitoring-evasion and stop-path tests if misalignment is in scope.\n- Pass criteria defined in advance in config or test code.\n- The release pipeline blocks promotion on any blocking failure and stores results keyed to the candidate manifest and model IDs.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Eval jobs marked `continue-on-error` or allowed to fail without blocking deploy.\n- Only happy-path or quality evals; no attack or denial cases.\n- Tests run against a mocked model or a different model ID than the release candidate.\n- Results not stored or not tied to a specific manifest.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- CI workflows and release pipelines\n- eval and test directories (red-team suites, promptfoo, inspect, pytest suites)\n- release gate scripts\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C06 run: suite version, candidate manifest and model IDs, results, and the gate decision for the exact release candidate.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C07 · G2 · C8/C9 — Rollback and restart\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nKeep an approved recovery setup and show that rollback works. Confirm each hosted model it names is still offered, and record the announced retirement date. Account for changed credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work. Don't restore revoked permissions or replay unsafe side effects just because the old artifact still exists.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nUpgrade a test deployment, create pending work and a revoked credential, then roll back. Confirm the approved old artifact runs and each hosted model it names is still offered. Confirm the credential stays revoked. Confirm queues, memory, search state, and side effects match the recovery procedure.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Rollback events, actual permissions, state and queue comparison.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C07.1** An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists.\n- **C07.2** Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how.\n- **C07.3** The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is.\n- **C07.4** Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Changed or tampered checkpoints accepted, or unsafe replay of actions → **R14**\n- Hosted model changing behind a fixed name → **C05**\n- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A documented rollback procedure and an approved previous release kept available (prior image digests, prior manifest).\n- Rollback steps that handle credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work.\n- Revocations stored outside the release artifact so a rollback does not restore them.\n- Retirement dates recorded for each hosted model the recovery release names.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Rollback redeploys an old manifest that includes credentials or permissions since revoked.\n- Queued jobs replay automatically after rollback with no check for already-done side effects.\n- Recovery release names a hosted model with no check that it is still offered.\n- No retained artifact for the previous release (images overwritten by tag).\n\n**Where to look:**\n- runbooks and rollback scripts\n- deploy pipeline rollback stages, Helm history, GitOps revisions\n- queue and memory/index migration code\n- secret and credential management config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C07 drill: upgrade, create pending work and a revoked credential, roll back, and confirm the credential stays revoked and state and queues match the procedure.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## C08 · G2 · Obs-T1/Obs-T2/Obs-T3 — Connect configuration to execution\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFrom a trace, look up the release manifest, the context sizes at each decision, and any prompts a parent passed to a sub-agent. Keep these links for the full audit retention period. Control access to sensitive artifacts.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nPick a past trace. Using the incident-response role, pull its manifest, model records, context sizes, and any prompts passed from a parent. Confirm these links stay available for the required retention period under the storage policy.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Lookup results, retention and access settings, missing-link check.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **C08.1** Each trace or audit record carries the agent-type-id or manifest reference that resolves to the stored release manifest.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the trace or audit record writer. 2) Check every record carries `agent.type.id` or a manifest reference. 3) Check that value is the key of a stored manifest. 4) Answer no if the field is missing on some paths (errors, background jobs) or holds a value that resolves to no stored manifest.\n- **C08.2** The context size at each model decision is recorded in the trace.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find model call sites and their spans. 2) Look for the context size recorded per call (for example `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` or a context-length attribute). 3) Answer no if it is missing on any model call path, including retries and sub-agents.\n- **C08.3** Prompts passed from a parent to a sub-agent are stored and linked from the trace.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code. 2) Check the parent's prompt is stored and the trace holds a link to it (ID, hash, or URI). 3) Answer no if the prompt is not stored, prompt capture is off, or the trace has no link.\n- **C08.4** Storage config keeps manifests, traces, and these links for at least the audit retention period, and limits access to sensitive artifacts by role.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find storage config for manifests, traces, and prompt links: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention, TTLs. 2) Check retention covers the audit period for all of them. 3) Check IAM limits sensitive artifacts by role. 4) Answer no if any has a shorter TTL, no retention, or broad read access. Answer unknown if storage is configured outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Context size not measured or logged for each action decision → **R07**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n- Manifest missing components that define the release → **C01**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Traces carry `agent.type.id` linking to a stored manifest, `agent.context.size` per decision, and `agent.parent.prompt` (or its hash reference) for sub-agents.\n- Manifest archive keyed by type ID with retention at least as long as the audit retention period.\n- Storage policies (bucket lifecycle, log retention settings) that match the required retention.\n- Access controls on sensitive artifacts such as prompts and parent prompts.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `agent.context.size` missing, or emitted as zero when unknown.\n- Manifests or prompts kept for less time than traces, so old traces can't be resolved.\n- Trace retention set to a short default (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer audit export.\n- Sensitive prompt artifacts readable by everyone with log access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and exporter config\n- log and trace backend retention settings, bucket lifecycle rules\n- manifest storage and artifact registry config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the C08 recipe: from a past trace, pull the manifest, model records, context sizes, and parent prompts with the incident-response role, and confirm retention settings.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n","numLines":517,"startLine":1,"totalLines":517}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfddpcPWKDbva1mzsEADG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YKjvQMaYzYNPuFzWfgRVTT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/ecosystem.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29680,"cache_read_input_tokens":145560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29680},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"fec1102a-2e05-4393-be7a-359c59178833","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:42.251Z","request_id":"req_011Cfddpc49rxPuQPe7NFzHc","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01YKjvQMaYzYNPuFzWfgRVTT":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/ecosystem.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YKjvQMaYzYNPuFzWfgRVTT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n2\t\n3\t# E — Ecosystem\n4\t\n5\tUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n6\t\n7\t## E01 · G2 · C1–C9 — Map shared responsibilities\n8\t\n9\t**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure, Vendors.\n10\t\n11\t### Requirement\n12\t\n13\tList the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\n14\t\n15\t### Verification recipe\n16\t\n17\tMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.\n18\t\n19\t**Evidence to keep:** Dependency map, responsibility table, evidence links.\n20\t\n21\t### Checks for likely_pass\n22\t\n23\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n24\t\n25\t- **E01.1** An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. *(A document can show this.)*\n26\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n27\t- **E01.2** Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. *(A document can show this.)*\n28\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n29\t- **E01.3** Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory. *(A document can show this.)*\n30\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n31\t\n32\t### Not covered here\n33\t\n34\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n35\t\n36\t- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n37\t- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n38\t- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n39\t\n40\t### Heuristics\n41\t\n42\t**Provable from code:** partly\n43\t\n44\t**Look for:**\n45\t- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n46\t- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n47\t- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n48\t\n49\t**Red flags:**\n50\t- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n51\t- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n52\t- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n53\t\n54\t**Where to look:**\n55\t- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n56\t- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n57\t- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n58\t\n59\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n60\t\n61\t**N/A when:** never\n62\t\n63\t## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n64\t\n65\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n66\t\n67\t### Requirement\n68\t\n69\tBefore approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n70\t\n71\t### Verification recipe\n72\t\n73\tFor each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n74\t\n75\t**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n76\t\n77\t### Checks for likely_pass\n78\t\n79\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n80\t\n81\t- **E02.1** Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n82\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n83\t- **E02.2** Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n84\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n85\t- **E02.3** A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n86\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n87\t- **E02.4** Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception. *(A document can show this.)*\n88\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n89\t\n90\t### Not covered here\n91\t\n92\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n93\t\n94\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n95\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n96\t- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n97\t- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n98\t\n99\t### Heuristics\n100\t\n101\t**Provable from code:** partly\n102\t\n103\t**Look for:**\n104\t- MCP servers, packages, and images pinned to exact versions or digests (`pkg@1.4.2`, `image@sha256:...`).\n105\t- Dependency scanning in CI (for example OSV-Scanner, Trivy, Grype, Dependabot) with a policy for findings.\n106\t- Signature or provenance checks (Sigstore/cosign, SLSA attestations, npm provenance) where available.\n107\t- Model files, adapters, and datasets included in the pinned list with revisions or digests.\n108\t- Review records for each approved tool or server: publisher, permissions, update source.\n109\t\n110\t**Red flags:**\n111\t- MCP server launched with `npx -y package` or `uvx package` without a pinned version.\n112\t- Remote MCP server URLs with no record of publisher review or limits.\n113\t- Docker images pulled by `:latest` for tools or servers.\n114\t- Hub models or datasets loaded by name with no revision pin.\n115\t- Registry namespace verification treated as proof the code is safe.\n116\t\n117\t**Where to look:**\n118\t- *.mcp.json, `claude_desktop_config.json`, agent tool registries\n119\t- lockfiles, Dockerfiles, compose and k8s manifests\n120\t- CI scanning and signing steps\n121\t- model and dataset download code\n122\t\n123\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Signature verification results and scan outputs come from the E02 run; code shows only whether pins and checks are configured.\n124\t\n125\t**N/A when:** never\n126\t\n127\t## E03 · G2 · C5 — Re-check tools on load\n128\t\n129\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Vendors.\n130\t\n131\t### Requirement\n132\t\n133\tTake a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\n134\t\n135\t### Verification recipe\n136\t\n137\tLoad an approved tool. Then, on a test server, change its description, schema, or implementation version, and refresh. Unexpected changes must block use until reviewed. That includes when a cached definition would otherwise be used after a failed re-check. Confirm that a metadata hash doesn't claim to catch changed remote code when the metadata stays the same. Record implementation pinning and visibility separately. Query which deployments use the affected dependency.\n138\t\n139\t**Evidence to keep:** Approved and new fingerprints, load denials, affected-deployment query.\n140\t\n141\t### Checks for likely_pass\n142\t\n143\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n144\t\n145\t- **E03.1** A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n146\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n147\t- **E03.2** The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n148\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n149\t- **E03.3** When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n150\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n151\t- **E03.4** The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n152\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n153\t\n154\t### Not covered here\n155\t\n156\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n157\t\n158\t- Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved → **E04**\n159\t- Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions → **E02**\n160\t- A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one → **E05**\n161\t- Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts → **E06**\n162\t\n163\t### Heuristics\n164\t\n165\t**Provable from code:** yes\n166\t\n167\t**Look for:**\n168\t- Stored fingerprints (hashes) of each approved tool's description and schema, compared on every load, cache refresh, and `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n169\t- Load is blocked when the fingerprint doesn't match or the re-check fails; no fallback to a cached definition.\n170\t- Implementation version pinned or attested separately from the metadata fingerprint where visible.\n171\t- A query or inventory showing which deployments use each tool or server version.\n172\t\n173\t**Red flags:**\n174\t- Tool lists fetched from MCP servers and passed to the model with no fingerprint comparison.\n175\t- `list_changed` notifications handled by silently reloading tools.\n176\t- On re-check error, the code falls back to the cached tool definition.\n177\t- Docs or code claim a metadata hash detects changes to remote server code.\n178\t\n179\t**Where to look:**\n180\t- MCP client code and tool registry or loader\n181\t- gateway tool-catalog config\n182\t- approved tool lists or lock files for tools\n183\t\n184\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code usually shows whether pinning and re-checks exist; the E03 recipe confirms that a changed description, schema, or version on a test server blocks use, including after a failed re-check.\n185\t\n186\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\n187\t\n188\t## E04 · G2 · C5 — Inspect tool metadata\n189\t\n190\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Infrastructure.\n191\t\n192\t### Requirement\n193\t\n194\tBefore the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\n195\t\n196\t  Why this is hard: Hidden instructions can be written in plain language, with no strange encoding or control characters. Scanners can also flag honest descriptions. A clean scan doesn't prove a tool is trustworthy.\n197\t\n198\t  If you can't fully do it: Use a small approved tool catalog, with reviewed, versioned descriptions and schemas. Block unexpected changes. Keep rule-based limits and known-pattern checks even without advanced scanning. Limit each tool's powers regardless of what its metadata says. Turn a tool off when the required inspection isn't available. Record what the scanner covers and misses. Manual review and pattern scans are useful layers. They don't prove every poisoned tool is caught.\n199\t\n200\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: A smaller catalog, review delays, and some honest metadata getting rejected. Limited scanning for attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 acceptance, as long as tool powers are limited separately. Known-pattern tests don't prove you can catch every poisoned tool.\n201\t\n202\t### Verification recipe\n203\t\n204\tAdd test fixtures with hidden or encoded instructions, and overlong descriptions, to tool metadata. Put them in the server instructions field, annotations, and prompt and resource templates too. Add a description that tells the model how to use another server's tool. Confirm the set rejection or cleaning happens before the model sees them. Turn off the scanner. Unchecked metadata must not get through.\n205\t\n206\t**Evidence to keep:** Metadata fixtures, scan results, check of what the model saw.\n207\t\n208\t### Checks for likely_pass\n209\t\n210\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n211\t\n212\t- **E04.1** A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n213\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n214\t- **E04.2** The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n215\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n216\t- **E04.3** Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n217\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n218\t- **E04.4** A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n219\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n220\t\n221\t### Not covered here\n222\t\n223\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n224\t\n225\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n226\t- Hidden instructions in tool call results → **R01**\n227\t- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n228\t- Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only → **B01**\n229\t\n230\t### Heuristics\n231\t\n232\t**Provable from code:** yes\n233\t\n234\t**Look for:**\n235\t- A scanner or filter that runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before they reach the model.\n236\t- Checks for hidden or control characters, encoded payloads, role overrides, references to other servers' tools, and length limits.\n237\t- Scanner errors cause rejection (fail closed), not pass-through.\n238\t- Where scanning is limited, a small approved catalog with reviewed, versioned metadata.\n239\t\n240\t**Red flags:**\n241\t- Tool descriptions and server `instructions` inserted into the system prompt unchanged.\n242\t- Only descriptions are scanned; schemas, annotations, or templates skip the scanner.\n243\t- Scanner wrapped in `try/except` that logs and continues on error.\n244\t- No length limits on tool metadata.\n245\t\n246\t**Where to look:**\n247\t- MCP client or gateway code that builds the tool list for the model\n248\t- metadata scanning or sanitizing modules\n249\t- gateway policy config\n250\t\n251\t**Needs runtime evidence:** The E04 recipe with poisoned metadata fixtures confirms what the model actually saw, including with the scanner turned off.\n252\t\n253\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\n254\t\n255\t## E05 · G2 · C2/C5 — Verified, namespaced tools\n256\t\n257\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n258\t\n259\t### Requirement\n260\t\n261\tIdentify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n262\t\n263\t### Verification recipe\n264\t\n265\tRegister a second test server with a duplicate or lookalike tool name. The registry must flag the conflict, and calls must go to the approved server identity. A matching bare name must not change the target. Register a server whose self-reported name copies an approved server's name. It must not inherit that server's approval.\n266\t\n267\t**Evidence to keep:** Registration warning, resolved tool identity, destination log.\n268\t\n269\t### Checks for likely_pass\n270\t\n271\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n272\t\n273\t- **E05.1** Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n274\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n275\t- **E05.2** The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n276\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n277\t- **E05.3** Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n278\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n279\t\n280\t### Not covered here\n281\t\n282\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n283\t\n284\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n285\t- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n286\t- Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message → **E07**\n287\t- Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action → **E06**\n288\t\n289\t### Heuristics\n290\t\n291\t**Provable from code:** yes\n292\t\n293\t**Look for:**\n294\t- Tools keyed by verified server identity plus tool name (for example a namespaced `server-id/tool`), where server identity comes from the endpoint, credential, or registry namespace.\n295\t- Registration code that flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n296\t- Approvals bound to verified server identity, not to the server's self-reported name.\n297\t\n298\t**Red flags:**\n299\t- Tools merged into one flat dict by bare name, so a later server overwrites an earlier one.\n300\t- Server identity taken from the `serverInfo.name` the server reports about itself.\n301\t- No warning when two servers expose the same or similar tool name.\n302\t\n303\t**Where to look:**\n304\t- tool registry and MCP client multiplexing code\n305\t- gateway routing config\n306\t- *.mcp.json server entries\n307\t\n308\t**Needs runtime evidence:** The E05 recipe registers a duplicate or lookalike server and confirms calls still go to the approved server identity.\n309\t\n310\t**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\n311\t\n312\t## E06 · G2 · C1/C2/C4/C5/C9 — Enforced gateway boundary\n313\t\n314\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team, Vendors.\n315\t\n316\t### Requirement\n317\t\n318\tSend MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n319\t\n320\t### Verification recipe\n321\t\n322\tMake allowed calls through the gateway. Then try going around it straight to the server, tokens for the wrong audience or that have expired, broken responses, and a scanner or policy outage. Send a `tools/call` whose `Mcp-Name` header names a different tool than the body. Request a cached tool list using a second caller's token. Test that downstream credentials are separate, that the issuer (`iss`) is checked where the gateway is an OAuth client, and that local stdio launches are restricted, where these apply. Unapproved and unchecked traffic must fail closed. Allowed traffic must still be logged, and rate limits must work.\n323\t\n324\t**Evidence to keep:** Route and policy settings, request and response fixtures, gateway decisions.\n325\t\n326\t### Checks for likely_pass\n327\t\n328\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n329\t\n330\t- **E06.1** The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n331\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n332\t- **E06.2** The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n333\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n334\t- **E06.3** The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n335\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n336\t- **E06.4** If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n337\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n338\t- **E06.5** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n339\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n340\t- **E06.6** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n341\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n342\t\n343\t### Not covered here\n344\t\n345\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n346\t\n347\t- Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool → **B01**\n348\t- Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions → **B02**\n349\t- Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval → **B05**\n350\t- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n351\t- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n352\t\n353\t### Heuristics\n354\t\n355\t**Provable from code:** partly\n356\t\n357\t**Look for:**\n358\t- All MCP traffic routed through a gateway or enforcement layer; network policy blocks direct paths from the agent to servers.\n359\t- Gateway checks token issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes; uses separate downstream credentials (token exchange) rather than passing the caller's token through.\n360\t- Per-call authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and decision logging.\n361\t- Rejection of requests whose MCP headers (for example `Mcp-Name`) don't match the body, before policy runs; tool-list caches keyed by authorization context.\n362\t- `iss` checked (RFC 9207) where the gateway is an OAuth client; SSRF protection on discovery URLs; stdio servers launched in a sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n363\t- Fail-closed settings when the policy engine or scanner is down.\n364\t\n365\t**Red flags:**\n366\t- Agent config lists MCP server URLs directly, bypassing the gateway, with egress open.\n367\t- Gateway forwards the incoming bearer token unchanged to downstream servers.\n368\t- Policy or scanner outage handled with fail-open (`on_error: allow`, catch and continue).\n369\t- Cached tool lists shared across callers or tenants.\n370\t- stdio servers launched on the host with full environment variables and network access.\n371\t\n372\t**Where to look:**\n373\t- gateway config (route, auth, policy, rate-limit rules)\n374\t- *.mcp.json and agent MCP client settings\n375\t- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy, firewall rules\n376\t- OAuth client and token-exchange config; sandbox or container settings for stdio servers\n377\t\n378\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E06 tests: bypass attempts, wrong-audience and expired tokens, broken responses, header-body mismatch, cross-caller cache reads, and policy or scanner outages must all fail closed.\n379\t\n380\t**N/A when:** The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\n381\t\n382\t## E07 · G2 · C2/C5 — Untrusted peer messages\n383\t\n384\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n385\t\n386\t### Requirement\n387\t\n388\tAuthenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n389\t\n390\t### Verification recipe\n391\t\n392\tSend valid, broken, fake-sender, replayed, and cross-tenant peer requests. Reused action requests must not repeat side effects without approval. Authenticate the sender of every request, even validly signed ones, and check its scope. Deny requests that aren't allowed, and keep hostile content as untrusted data. Include a schema-valid injected instruction, a tampered signed agent card, and a callback URL that points at a private or metadata address.\n393\t\n394\t**Evidence to keep:** Peer policy, message fixtures, authorization and validation logs.\n395\t\n396\t### Checks for likely_pass\n397\t\n398\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n399\t\n400\t- **E07.1** Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n401\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n402\t- **E07.2** Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n403\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n404\t- **E07.3** Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n405\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n406\t- **E07.4** Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n407\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n408\t- **E07.5** Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n409\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n410\t\n411\t### Not covered here\n412\t\n413\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n414\t\n415\t- Tool server identified only by its self-reported name → **E05**\n416\t- Missing schema or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n417\t- Peers or children still running after a stop → **E09**\n418\t- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n419\t\n420\t### Heuristics\n421\t\n422\t**Provable from code:** partly\n423\t\n424\t**Look for:**\n425\t- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n426\t- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n427\t- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n428\t- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n429\t- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n430\t- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n431\t\n432\t**Red flags:**\n433\t- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n434\t- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n435\t- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n436\t- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n437\t- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n438\t\n439\t**Where to look:**\n440\t- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n441\t- peer auth middleware\n442\t- webhook and callback handlers\n443\t- idempotency and replay protection code\n444\t\n445\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n446\t\n447\t**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n448\t\n449\t## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n450\t\n451\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n452\t\n453\t### Requirement\n454\t\n455\tGive sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\n456\t\n457\t  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n458\t\n459\t  If you can't fully do it: Carry the verified original identity, tenant, and allowed scope all the way to where the action runs. If a peer can't enforce those limits, delegate only to peers with no more relevant authority. Or use a narrow task credential, or require separate approval for that exact privileged action. If none of these can be enforced, turn off that delegation path. Trusting a peer's prompt does not pass this blocking check.\n460\t\n461\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less flexible delegation, narrower child roles, or separate approval for each action. These keep the authorization boundary intact. Quiet privilege gains can never pass this G1 check. Turn off the path if you can't enforce the boundary.\n462\t\n463\t### Verification recipe\n464\t\n465\tGive parent A a read-only task, and ask it to hand a forbidden write to a more powerful peer. The handed-off write must be denied without its own valid approval. A legitimate handed-off task that is clearly approved must still work. Present the parent's token to the child's service. It must be rejected.\n466\t\n467\t**Evidence to keep:** Parent and child grants, delegation decisions, destination state.\n468\t\n469\t### Checks for likely_pass\n470\t\n471\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n472\t\n473\t- **E08.1** Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n474\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n475\t- **E08.2** Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n476\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n477\t- **E08.3** An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n478\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n479\t- **E08.4** Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n480\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n481\t\n482\t### Not covered here\n483\t\n484\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n485\t\n486\t- Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments → **B06**\n487\t- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n488\t- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n489\t- Replayed or faked peer-agent messages → **E07**\n490\t- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n491\t\n492\t### Heuristics\n493\t\n494\t**Provable from code:** partly\n495\t\n496\t**Look for:**\n497\t- Each sub-agent gets its own narrowed role or credential (token exchange per RFC 8693, transaction tokens, down-scoped tokens) at each hop.\n498\t- Authorization uses the original subject and the current actor; earlier actors in the chain are recorded only.\n499\t- Children cannot be given powers the parent lacks unless there is an explicit, separate approval.\n500\t- Audience-restricted tokens, so a parent's token is rejected by a child's service.\n501\t\n502\t**Red flags:**\n503\t- Parent passes its own full credentials or API keys to sub-agents.\n504\t- A low-privilege agent can call a more powerful peer that acts with the peer's own broad credentials.\n505\t- Authorization decided from the `act` chain or a peer-supplied delegator claim instead of the original subject.\n506\t- Tokens with no audience restriction reused across services.\n507\t\n508\t**Where to look:**\n509\t- sub-agent spawn and handoff code\n510\t- token exchange and credential issuing code\n511\t- peer agent authorization policy\n512\t- IAM role trust policies for agent roles\n513\t\n514\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E08 test: a read-only parent asking a more powerful peer for a forbidden write must be denied, a properly approved handoff must work, and the parent's token must be rejected at the child's service.\n515\t\n516\t**N/A when:** The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\n517\t\n518\t## E09 · G1 · C8 — Recursive shutdown\n519\t\n520\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n521\t\n522\t### Requirement\n523\t\n524\tRun a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n525\t\n526\t  Why this is hard: Remote workers, disconnected children, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks can outlive the parent. A stop message can arrive late or get lost. Remote effects that already happened may not be cancelable.\n527\t\n528\t  If you can't fully do it: Combine stop signals that spread down the tree with revocation, time limits on tasks, and short-lived leases that must be renewed to keep working. Block new work from being sent. Test that disconnected workers lose the ability to act within the stop time limit. Decide separately how long-running calls get canceled or reconciled. Leave out remote execution paths that can't meet the time limit. Record completed effects separately and reconcile them under R12.\n529\t\n530\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer remote workers, the overhead of renewing leases, and slower completion. A set stop time is fine when it is tested and fits the potential harm. A worker that keeps its authority past that time is a blocking gap.\n531\t\n532\t### Verification recipe\n533\t\n534\tBuild a test tree with nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Stop the root. Try delayed retries and new calls from descendants. Confirm every descendant is stopped or revoked within the time limit, and finished effects are reconciled.\n535\t\n536\t**Evidence to keep:** Full tree, stop and revocation times, retry denials, reconciliation log.\n537\t\n538\t### Checks for likely_pass\n539\t\n540\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n541\t\n542\t- **E09.1** A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n543\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n544\t- **E09.2** Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n545\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n546\t- **E09.3** Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n547\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n548\t- **E09.4** Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n549\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n550\t\n551\t### Not covered here\n552\t\n553\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n554\t\n555\t- Agent able to disable or delay its own stop → **R11**\n556\t- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n557\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n558\t- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n559\t\n560\t### Heuristics\n561\t\n562\t**Provable from code:** partly\n563\t\n564\t**Look for:**\n565\t- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n566\t- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n567\t- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n568\t- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n569\t\n570\t**Red flags:**\n571\t- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n572\t- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n573\t- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n574\t- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n575\t\n576\t**Where to look:**\n577\t- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n578\t- queue consumers and retry config\n579\t- credential TTLs and lease logic\n580\t- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n581\t\n582\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n583\t\n584\t**N/A when:** never\n585\t\n586\t## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n587\t\n588\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n589\t\n590\t### Requirement\n591\t\n592\tRebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n593\t\n594\t  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n595\t\n596\t  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n597\t\n598\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n599\t\n600\t### Verification recipe\n601\t\n602\tRun a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n603\t\n604\t**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n605\t\n606\t### Checks for likely_pass\n607\t\n608\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n609\t\n610\t- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n611\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n612\t- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n613\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n614\t- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n615\t  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n616\t- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n617\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n618\t\n619\t### Not covered here\n620\t\n621\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n622\t\n623\t- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n624\t- Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record → **R13**\n625\t- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n626\t- Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident → **R14**\n627\t\n628\t### Heuristics\n629\t\n630\t**Provable from code:** partly\n631\t\n632\t**Look for:**\n633\t- Gateway, tool services, and workers all emit the six identity fields and propagate W3C trace context.\n634\t- Gateway maps verified identity to vendor job or receipt IDs where identity can't pass downstream.\n635\t- Audit logs go to a sink the agent can only append to (separate write-only role, WORM storage, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode).\n636\t- No tool or peer the agent can call has delete or edit rights on audit storage.\n637\t\n638\t**Red flags:**\n639\t- Audit logs written by the agent's own credentials to a bucket it can delete from.\n640\t- A tool or admin API the agent can call that can delete or rewrite logs.\n641\t- Vendor calls made with shared credentials and no mapping from receipts back to agent and instance.\n642\t- Trace context dropped at the gateway or at queue boundaries.\n643\t\n644\t**Where to look:**\n645\t- audit log sink config and its IAM policy\n646\t- bucket or storage settings (object lock, retention mode)\n647\t- gateway logging and trace propagation\n648\t- tool definitions with admin or log-management powers\n649\t\n650\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E10 test: merge logs across services into one graph with all six fields, and show deletes or edits by the agent, directly or through a more powerful tool or peer, are denied or held for approval.\n651\t\n652\t**N/A when:** never\n653\t\n654\t## E11 · G2 · C9 — Protect shared evidence\n655\t\n656\t**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Governance, Data and privacy, Security, Vendors.\n657\t\n658\t### Requirement\n659\t\n660\tSet rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n661\t\n662\t  Why this is hard: Audit delivery can fail while the agent keeps working. Buffers can fill up, records can arrive late, and a vendor may keep records for less time than you need. Exported logs may also hold sensitive data.\n663\t\n664\t  If you can't fully do it: Use durable local buffering, or another protected audit path, with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts for missing records. Export vendor evidence before the vendor deletes it, and limit who can see it. When buffering runs out or isn't available, pause affected actions before required evidence is lost. Record outages and any evidence you can't get back. A fixed pipeline doesn't bring back missing history.\n665\t\n666\t  Tradeoffs you can accept: Short delivery delays while records stay safely stored, extra storage and export costs, and paused work during outages. Limits on retention or delivery can get G2 acceptance only if the required G1 action capture and attribution still hold. Disclose any lost evidence.\n667\t\n668\t### Verification recipe\n669\t\n670\tPull up an old test trace, check its integrity, and run the export and recovery procedure. Interrupt audit delivery and inject a missing event in the sequence. The gap must be caught, and high-impact actions must fail closed while capture is down. Check the retention period against the investigation window and the law that applies.\n671\t\n672\t**Evidence to keep:** Retention and access policy, integrity and export results, outage alerts and response.\n673\t\n674\t### Checks for likely_pass\n675\t\n676\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n677\t\n678\t- **E11.1** Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n679\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n680\t- **E11.2** Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n681\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n682\t- **E11.3** Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n683\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n684\t- **E11.4** High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n685\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n686\t\n687\t### Not covered here\n688\t\n689\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n690\t\n691\t- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n692\t- Run record missing denials, approvals, or results → **R13**\n693\t- Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs → **R03**\n694\t\n695\t### Heuristics\n696\t\n697\t**Provable from code:** partly\n698\t\n699\t**Look for:**\n700\t- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n701\t- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n702\t- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n703\t- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n704\t- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n705\t\n706\t**Red flags:**\n707\t- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n708\t- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n709\t- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n710\t- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n711\t\n712\t**Where to look:**\n713\t- audit logging client and buffering code\n714\t- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n715\t- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n716\t- data retention and redaction policy docs\n717\t\n718\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n719\t\n720\t**N/A when:** never\n721\t\n722\t## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n723\t\n724\t**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n725\t\n726\t### Requirement\n727\t\n728\tRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n729\t\n730\t### Verification recipe\n731\t\n732\tMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n733\t\n734\t**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n735\t\n736\t### Checks for likely_pass\n737\t\n738\tChecks for likely_pass:\n739\t\n740\t- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n741\t\n742\t### Not covered here\n743\t\n744\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n745\t\n746\t- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n747\t- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n748\t- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n749\t- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n750\t\n751\t### Heuristics\n752\t\n753\t**Provable from code:** no\n754\t\n755\t**Look for:**\n756\t- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n757\t- Quarantine controls for a shared tool or agent type (gateway deny rule, registry block, feature flag).\n758\t- Alert routes for cross-agent abuse and budget alerts to a named responder team.\n759\t- An incident runbook that names who decides on outside reporting and information sharing.\n760\t\n761\t**Red flags:**\n762\t- No way to find which deployments or tenants use a given tool version.\n763\t- Quarantine requires a code change and full redeploy.\n764\t- Alerts go to an unowned channel or a single person.\n765\t- No named decision-maker for outside reporting.\n766\t\n767\t**Where to look:**\n768\t- incident runbooks and on-call config\n769\t- alert routing config (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Alertmanager)\n770\t- gateway and tool registry block rules\n771\t- SBOM or dependency inventory systems\n772\t\n773\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Almost all of this item is shown by the E12 drill: mark a shared tool compromised, query the impact, quarantine, revoke, alert, and measure stop times. Code only shows that the controls exist.\n774\t\n775\t**N/A when:** never\n776\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be046cb2-a615-4b99-9b5b-9574ded4eee7","uuid":"3bf8777c-91f5-41e7-b34d-6528ebb6b147","timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:19:42.538Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-_3kdytd7/skill/references/ecosystem.md","content":"<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# E — Ecosystem\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## E01 · G2 · C1–C9 — Map shared responsibilities\n\n**Lead:** Governance. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Dependency map, responsibility table, evidence links.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E01.1** An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.\n- **E01.2** Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.\n- **E01.3** Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n- A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records → **E10**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A dependency map listing outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.\n- A responsibility table naming the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner for each applicable control, with evidence links for both.\n- Owner records in machine-readable form (service catalog, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles, CODEOWNERS).\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Outside services in config (MCP servers, API base URLs, webhooks) that do not appear in any dependency or ownership list.\n- Controls marked N/A because an upstream vendor provides the service.\n- Vendor evidence assumed rather than linked, with no gap recorded.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- *.mcp.json, agent config, env vars with service URLs\n- service catalog files, architecture docs, `.brace/` records\n- IaC for gateways, identity providers, and log sinks\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows declared dependencies; the E01 recipe compares them with a test trace of real calls and links evidence for each owner.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E02 · G2 · C3/C5 — Vet and pin dependencies\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Agent team, Model team, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nBefore approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nFor each approved dependency, including model files, adapters, and datasets, find the publisher, artifact and version, permissions, and update source. Verify signatures where they exist. Scan the pinned list for known vulnerabilities, and judge the findings under the team's release policy. Record missing provenance and how you reduce the risk.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Dependency list, signature and scan results, review and exception records.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E02.1** Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL.\n- **E02.2** Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it.\n- **E02.3** A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used.\n- **E02.4** Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception. *(A document can show this.)*\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n- Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked → **B10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- MCP servers, packages, and images pinned to exact versions or digests (`pkg@1.4.2`, `image@sha256:...`).\n- Dependency scanning in CI (for example OSV-Scanner, Trivy, Grype, Dependabot) with a policy for findings.\n- Signature or provenance checks (Sigstore/cosign, SLSA attestations, npm provenance) where available.\n- Model files, adapters, and datasets included in the pinned list with revisions or digests.\n- Review records for each approved tool or server: publisher, permissions, update source.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- MCP server launched with `npx -y package` or `uvx package` without a pinned version.\n- Remote MCP server URLs with no record of publisher review or limits.\n- Docker images pulled by `:latest` for tools or servers.\n- Hub models or datasets loaded by name with no revision pin.\n- Registry namespace verification treated as proof the code is safe.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- *.mcp.json, `claude_desktop_config.json`, agent tool registries\n- lockfiles, Dockerfiles, compose and k8s manifests\n- CI scanning and signing steps\n- model and dataset download code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Signature verification results and scan outputs come from the E02 run; code shows only whether pins and checks are configured.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E03 · G2 · C5 — Re-check tools on load\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTake a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nLoad an approved tool. Then, on a test server, change its description, schema, or implementation version, and refresh. Unexpected changes must block use until reviewed. That includes when a cached definition would otherwise be used after a failed re-check. Confirm that a metadata hash doesn't claim to catch changed remote code when the metadata stays the same. Record implementation pinning and visibility separately. Query which deployments use the affected dependency.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Approved and new fingerprints, load denials, affected-deployment query.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E03.1** A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\n- **E03.2** The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\n- **E03.3** When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load.\n- **E03.4** The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved → **E04**\n- Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions → **E02**\n- A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one → **E05**\n- Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Stored fingerprints (hashes) of each approved tool's description and schema, compared on every load, cache refresh, and `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n- Load is blocked when the fingerprint doesn't match or the re-check fails; no fallback to a cached definition.\n- Implementation version pinned or attested separately from the metadata fingerprint where visible.\n- A query or inventory showing which deployments use each tool or server version.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool lists fetched from MCP servers and passed to the model with no fingerprint comparison.\n- `list_changed` notifications handled by silently reloading tools.\n- On re-check error, the code falls back to the cached tool definition.\n- Docs or code claim a metadata hash detects changes to remote server code.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- MCP client code and tool registry or loader\n- gateway tool-catalog config\n- approved tool lists or lock files for tools\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code usually shows whether pinning and re-checks exist; the E03 recipe confirms that a changed description, schema, or version on a test server blocks use, including after a failed re-check.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.\n\n## E04 · G2 · C5 — Inspect tool metadata\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nBefore the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.\n\n  Why this is hard: Hidden instructions can be written in plain language, with no strange encoding or control characters. Scanners can also flag honest descriptions. A clean scan doesn't prove a tool is trustworthy.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use a small approved tool catalog, with reviewed, versioned descriptions and schemas. Block unexpected changes. Keep rule-based limits and known-pattern checks even without advanced scanning. Limit each tool's powers regardless of what its metadata says. Turn a tool off when the required inspection isn't available. Record what the scanner covers and misses. Manual review and pattern scans are useful layers. They don't prove every poisoned tool is caught.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: A smaller catalog, review delays, and some honest metadata getting rejected. Limited scanning for attacks hidden in meaning can get G2 acceptance, as long as tool powers are limited separately. Known-pattern tests don't prove you can catch every poisoned tool.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nAdd test fixtures with hidden or encoded instructions, and overlong descriptions, to tool metadata. Put them in the server instructions field, annotations, and prompt and resource templates too. Add a description that tells the model how to use another server's tool. Confirm the set rejection or cleaning happens before the model sees them. Turn off the scanner. Unchecked metadata must not get through.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Metadata fixtures, scan results, check of what the model saw.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E04.1** A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner.\n- **E04.2** The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing.\n- **E04.3** Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through.\n- **E04.4** A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Hidden instructions in tool call results → **R01**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n- Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only → **B01**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- A scanner or filter that runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before they reach the model.\n- Checks for hidden or control characters, encoded payloads, role overrides, references to other servers' tools, and length limits.\n- Scanner errors cause rejection (fail closed), not pass-through.\n- Where scanning is limited, a small approved catalog with reviewed, versioned metadata.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool descriptions and server `instructions` inserted into the system prompt unchanged.\n- Only descriptions are scanned; schemas, annotations, or templates skip the scanner.\n- Scanner wrapped in `try/except` that logs and continues on error.\n- No length limits on tool metadata.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- MCP client or gateway code that builds the tool list for the model\n- metadata scanning or sanitizing modules\n- gateway policy config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** The E04 recipe with poisoned metadata fixtures confirms what the model actually saw, including with the scanner turned off.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.\n\n## E05 · G2 · C2/C5 — Verified, namespaced tools\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIdentify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRegister a second test server with a duplicate or lookalike tool name. The registry must flag the conflict, and calls must go to the approved server identity. A matching bare name must not change the target. Register a server whose self-reported name copies an approved server's name. It must not inherit that server's approval.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Registration warning, resolved tool identity, destination log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E05.1** Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name.\n- **E05.2** The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates.\n- **E05.3** Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message → **E07**\n- Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** yes\n\n**Look for:**\n- Tools keyed by verified server identity plus tool name (for example a namespaced `server-id/tool`), where server identity comes from the endpoint, credential, or registry namespace.\n- Registration code that flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n- Approvals bound to verified server identity, not to the server's self-reported name.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tools merged into one flat dict by bare name, so a later server overwrites an earlier one.\n- Server identity taken from the `serverInfo.name` the server reports about itself.\n- No warning when two servers expose the same or similar tool name.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- tool registry and MCP client multiplexing code\n- gateway routing config\n- *.mcp.json server entries\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** The E05 recipe registers a duplicate or lookalike server and confirms calls still go to the approved server identity.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.\n\n## E06 · G2 · C1/C2/C4/C5/C9 — Enforced gateway boundary\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSend MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMake allowed calls through the gateway. Then try going around it straight to the server, tokens for the wrong audience or that have expired, broken responses, and a scanner or policy outage. Send a `tools/call` whose `Mcp-Name` header names a different tool than the body. Request a cached tool list using a second caller's token. Test that downstream credentials are separate, that the issuer (`iss`) is checked where the gateway is an OAuth client, and that local stdio launches are restricted, where these apply. Unapproved and unchecked traffic must fail closed. Allowed traffic must still be logged, and rate limits must work.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Route and policy settings, request and response fixtures, gateway decisions.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E06.1** The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n- **E06.2** The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n- **E06.3** The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n- **E06.4** If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n- **E06.5** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n- **E06.6** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool → **B01**\n- Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions → **B02**\n- Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval → **B05**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata → **E04**\n- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- All MCP traffic routed through a gateway or enforcement layer; network policy blocks direct paths from the agent to servers.\n- Gateway checks token issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes; uses separate downstream credentials (token exchange) rather than passing the caller's token through.\n- Per-call authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and decision logging.\n- Rejection of requests whose MCP headers (for example `Mcp-Name`) don't match the body, before policy runs; tool-list caches keyed by authorization context.\n- `iss` checked (RFC 9207) where the gateway is an OAuth client; SSRF protection on discovery URLs; stdio servers launched in a sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n- Fail-closed settings when the policy engine or scanner is down.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Agent config lists MCP server URLs directly, bypassing the gateway, with egress open.\n- Gateway forwards the incoming bearer token unchanged to downstream servers.\n- Policy or scanner outage handled with fail-open (`on_error: allow`, catch and continue).\n- Cached tool lists shared across callers or tenants.\n- stdio servers launched on the host with full environment variables and network access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- gateway config (route, auth, policy, rate-limit rules)\n- *.mcp.json and agent MCP client settings\n- k8s NetworkPolicy, egress proxy, firewall rules\n- OAuth client and token-exchange config; sandbox or container settings for stdio servers\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E06 tests: bypass attempts, wrong-audience and expired tokens, broken responses, header-body mismatch, cross-caller cache reads, and policy or scanner outages must all fail closed.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.\n\n## E07 · G2 · C2/C5 — Untrusted peer messages\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAuthenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSend valid, broken, fake-sender, replayed, and cross-tenant peer requests. Reused action requests must not repeat side effects without approval. Authenticate the sender of every request, even validly signed ones, and check its scope. Deny requests that aren't allowed, and keep hostile content as untrusted data. Include a schema-valid injected instruction, a tampered signed agent card, and a callback URL that points at a private or metadata address.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Peer policy, message fixtures, authorization and validation logs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E07.1** Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\n- **E07.2** Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\n- **E07.3** Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\n- **E07.4** Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\n- **E07.5** Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Tool server identified only by its self-reported name → **E05**\n- Missing schema or size checks on API responses → **R01**\n- Peers or children still running after a stop → **E09**\n- Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers → **E10**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Every peer message authenticated (mTLS, signed requests, verified tokens) and the sender's scope checked.\n- Message type and schema validation for incoming A2A or peer requests.\n- Nonce, request ID, or timestamp tracking to stop replays where an action could repeat.\n- Signed agent cards verified, and card claims not used as authorization.\n- Callback and webhook URLs checked against private, link-local, and metadata addresses (SSRF).\n- Peer message content passed to the model as untrusted data.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Peer requests accepted based on a valid signature alone, with no per-peer authorization.\n- Agent card skills or capabilities treated as permission grants.\n- Callback URLs fetched without SSRF checks (for example to `169.254.169.254`).\n- Retried peer requests re-run side effects with no idempotency key.\n- Peer message text inserted into the system prompt as instructions.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- A2A server handlers, agent card files (`/.well-known/agent-card.json`)\n- peer auth middleware\n- webhook and callback handlers\n- idempotency and replay protection code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E07 fixtures: fake-sender, replayed, cross-tenant, tampered-card, and private-callback requests must be denied.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).\n\n## E08 · G1 · C2/C4 — Bounded delegated authority\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Infrastructure, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nGive sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.\n\n  Why this is hard: A powerful peer may treat a low-privilege agent's request as a normal task. Knowing who sent the last message doesn't prove what the original requester was allowed to do. Delegation limits can also get lost across several hops.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Carry the verified original identity, tenant, and allowed scope all the way to where the action runs. If a peer can't enforce those limits, delegate only to peers with no more relevant authority. Or use a narrow task credential, or require separate approval for that exact privileged action. If none of these can be enforced, turn off that delegation path. Trusting a peer's prompt does not pass this blocking check.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Less flexible delegation, narrower child roles, or separate approval for each action. These keep the authorization boundary intact. Quiet privilege gains can never pass this G1 check. Turn off the path if you can't enforce the boundary.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nGive parent A a read-only task, and ask it to hand a forbidden write to a more powerful peer. The handed-off write must be denied without its own valid approval. A legitimate handed-off task that is clearly approved must still work. Present the parent's token to the child's service. It must be rejected.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Parent and child grants, delegation decisions, destination state.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E08.1** Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.\n- **E08.2** Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions.\n- **E08.3** An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check.\n- **E08.4** Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments → **B06**\n- Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded → **A06**\n- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n- Replayed or faked peer-agent messages → **E07**\n- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Each sub-agent gets its own narrowed role or credential (token exchange per RFC 8693, transaction tokens, down-scoped tokens) at each hop.\n- Authorization uses the original subject and the current actor; earlier actors in the chain are recorded only.\n- Children cannot be given powers the parent lacks unless there is an explicit, separate approval.\n- Audience-restricted tokens, so a parent's token is rejected by a child's service.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Parent passes its own full credentials or API keys to sub-agents.\n- A low-privilege agent can call a more powerful peer that acts with the peer's own broad credentials.\n- Authorization decided from the `act` chain or a peer-supplied delegator claim instead of the original subject.\n- Tokens with no audience restriction reused across services.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- sub-agent spawn and handoff code\n- token exchange and credential issuing code\n- peer agent authorization policy\n- IAM role trust policies for agent roles\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E08 test: a read-only parent asking a more powerful peer for a forbidden write must be denied, a properly approved handoff must work, and the parent's token must be rejected at the child's service.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.\n\n## E09 · G1 · C8 — Recursive shutdown\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Agent team, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n\n  Why this is hard: Remote workers, disconnected children, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks can outlive the parent. A stop message can arrive late or get lost. Remote effects that already happened may not be cancelable.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Combine stop signals that spread down the tree with revocation, time limits on tasks, and short-lived leases that must be renewed to keep working. Block new work from being sent. Test that disconnected workers lose the ability to act within the stop time limit. Decide separately how long-running calls get canceled or reconciled. Leave out remote execution paths that can't meet the time limit. Record completed effects separately and reconcile them under R12.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Fewer remote workers, the overhead of renewing leases, and slower completion. A set stop time is fine when it is tested and fits the potential harm. A worker that keeps its authority past that time is a blocking gap.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nBuild a test tree with nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Stop the root. Try delayed retries and new calls from descendants. Confirm every descendant is stopped or revoked within the time limit, and finished effects are reconciled.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Full tree, stop and revocation times, retry denials, reconciliation log.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E09.1** A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read.\n- **E09.2** Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority.\n- **E09.3** Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check.\n- **E09.4** Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent able to disable or delay its own stop → **R11**\n- No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user → **B04**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A stop signal that spreads from root to all descendants (cancellation tokens, propagated stop messages).\n- Short-lived leases or credentials that children must renew, so disconnected workers lose access.\n- Queued work for a stopped tree is dropped or blocked; retries check a stop flag before running.\n- Open tool streams, subscriptions, and remote tasks are canceled on stop; a reconciliation step for effects already done.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Stop only kills the parent process; children, queue workers, or remote tasks keep running.\n- Long-lived credentials given to remote workers.\n- Retry loops and schedulers with no check for a stopped run.\n- No time limit on tasks or subscriptions.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- orchestration and sub-agent lifecycle code\n- queue consumers and retry config\n- credential TTLs and lease logic\n- stop or kill endpoints and runbooks\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E09 drill across nested agents, a remote worker, queued work, and open streams, with stop and revocation times and denied retries.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E10 · G1 · C9/Obs-T1 — Audit across service boundaries\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Operations, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.\n\n  Why this is hard: Vendor services may drop parent or tenant identity, or return only request receipts. That leaves gaps between your gateway trace and what happened downstream. Shared credentials make attribution even weaker.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Keep verified identity and correlation IDs at your gateway. Map them to vendor job or receipt IDs, and match them against any destination records you can get. Where identity can't be passed downstream, use separate, narrow credentials. Record which parts of the history you can't inspect. Gateway logs alone can't prove what happened inside those services. Limit or replace integrations whose evidence can't meet the attribution requirement. Don't mark the whole graph complete.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Extra integration work, slower reconciliation, and leaving out services you can't see into. Mapping verified identity to vendor receipts can support attribution once you've shown it works end to end. Actions that can never be traced still block release.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nRun a task across the gateway, tools, and workers. Merge their logs into one graph with the six identity fields. Try to delete or edit records with the agent's credentials, and by asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Both must be denied, or held for approval under B05. Record any vendor segment you can't access as a gap.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Cross-service graph, completeness query, audit-write denial.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E10.1** The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo.\n- **E10.2** The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo.\n- **E10.3** No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05.\n- **E10.4** Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing → **E11**\n- Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record → **R13**\n- Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies → **A04**\n- Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident → **R14**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Gateway, tool services, and workers all emit the six identity fields and propagate W3C trace context.\n- Gateway maps verified identity to vendor job or receipt IDs where identity can't pass downstream.\n- Audit logs go to a sink the agent can only append to (separate write-only role, WORM storage, S3 Object Lock in compliance mode).\n- No tool or peer the agent can call has delete or edit rights on audit storage.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit logs written by the agent's own credentials to a bucket it can delete from.\n- A tool or admin API the agent can call that can delete or rewrite logs.\n- Vendor calls made with shared credentials and no mapping from receipts back to agent and instance.\n- Trace context dropped at the gateway or at queue boundaries.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit log sink config and its IAM policy\n- bucket or storage settings (object lock, retention mode)\n- gateway logging and trace propagation\n- tool definitions with admin or log-management powers\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E10 test: merge logs across services into one graph with all six fields, and show deletes or edits by the agent, directly or through a more powerful tool or peer, are denied or held for approval.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E11 · G2 · C9 — Protect shared evidence\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Governance, Data and privacy, Security, Vendors.\n\n### Requirement\n\nSet rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.\n\n  Why this is hard: Audit delivery can fail while the agent keeps working. Buffers can fill up, records can arrive late, and a vendor may keep records for less time than you need. Exported logs may also hold sensitive data.\n\n  If you can't fully do it: Use durable local buffering, or another protected audit path, with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts for missing records. Export vendor evidence before the vendor deletes it, and limit who can see it. When buffering runs out or isn't available, pause affected actions before required evidence is lost. Record outages and any evidence you can't get back. A fixed pipeline doesn't bring back missing history.\n\n  Tradeoffs you can accept: Short delivery delays while records stay safely stored, extra storage and export costs, and paused work during outages. Limits on retention or delivery can get G2 acceptance only if the required G1 action capture and attribution still hold. Disclose any lost evidence.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nPull up an old test trace, check its integrity, and run the export and recovery procedure. Interrupt audit delivery and inject a missing event in the sequence. The gap must be caught, and high-impact actions must fail closed while capture is down. Check the retention period against the investigation window and the law that applies.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Retention and access policy, integrity and export results, outage alerts and response.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **E11.1** Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo.\n- **E11.2** Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists.\n- **E11.3** Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified.\n- **E11.4** High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history → **E10**\n- Run record missing denials, approvals, or results → **R13**\n- Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs → **R03**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit logging client and buffering code\n- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n- data retention and redaction policy docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n- Quarantine controls for a shared tool or agent type (gateway deny rule, registry block, feature flag).\n- Alert routes for cross-agent abuse and budget alerts to a named responder team.\n- An incident runbook that names who decides on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No way to find which deployments or tenants use a given tool version.\n- Quarantine requires a code change and full redeploy.\n- Alerts go to an unowned channel or a single person.\n- No named decision-maker for outside reporting.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- incident runbooks and on-call config\n- alert routing config (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Alertmanager)\n- gateway and tool registry block rules\n- SBOM or dependency inventory systems\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Almost all of this item is shown by the E12 drill: mark a shared tool compromised, query the impact, quarantine, revoke, alert, and measure stop times. Code only shows that the controls exist.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n","numLines":776,"startLine":1,"totalLines":776}}}
