ault-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\",\n          \"met\": \"yes\",\n          \"evidence\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml:168\",\n          \"quote\": \"podSelector: {}\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"Read the whole NetworkPolicy: policyTypes Egress and Ingress with podSelector {} selects every pod; egress allows only kube-dns, the platform egress proxy on 3128 and 10.60.12.0/28:443; ingress only from the ingress namespace on 8080. No other policy in the repo widens it.\",\n          \"note\": \"Default-deny egress and ingress with named allowed peers.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"B08.2\",\n          \"check\": \"No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\",\n          \"met\": \"yes\",\n          \"evidence\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml:63\",\n          \"quote\": \"hostNetwork: false\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"Searched manifests for 0.0.0.0/