r's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\",\n      \"not_covered\": [\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Data leaking through client-side Markdown images or link previews\",\n          \"owner\": \"R03\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Network path to internal production or admin systems\",\n          \"owner\": \"B08\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"A loosened egress rule in the running setup raising no alert\",\n          \"owner\": \"C04\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"rating\": \"gap\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"file\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml\",\n          \"line\": 193,\n          \"note\": \"Direct routes around the proxy are blocked by NetworkPolicy, not only by env vars.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"B09.1\",\n          \"check\": \"Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\",\n          \"met\": \"unknown\",\n          \"evidence\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml:189\",\n          \"quote\": \"app: egress-proxy\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"\",\n          \"note\": \"NetworkPolicy is default-deny and allows only DNS, the platform egress proxy and the EHR /28. The destination allowlist itself lives in the platform proxy, outside this repo (deployment.yaml:160-161 only describes it).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"B09.2\",\n          \"check\": \"Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\",\n          \"met\": \"yes\",\n          \"evidence\": \"k8s/deployment.yaml:193\",\n          \"quote\": \"ipBlock: { cidr: 10.60.12.0/28 }\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"Read all egress rules: the only IP block is a private /28, no IPv6 range or 0.0.0.0/0, so IPv4 and IPv6 direct routes are denied by default. HTTPS_PROXY (deployment.yaml:43) is only a hint; NO_PROXY (line 44) lists the EHR host and .svc.cluster.local, which the policy still blocks except the EHR /28.\",\n          \"note\": \"Direct routes around the proxy are blocked by NetworkPolicy, not only by env vars.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"B09.3\",\n          \"check\": \"Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\",\n          \"met\": \"no\",\n          \"evidence\": \"src/integrations/eligibility-a2a.ts:83\",\n          \"quote\": \"const keys = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(header.jku));\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"\",\n          \"note\": \"The app fetches a key-set URL taken from the peer's own response header, with no host allowlist, redirect check, or private/metadata address check. Other fetches use configured hosts (fetc