dit or osv scan that fails the build and record publisher, permissions and update source per dependency.\",\n      \"lead_role\": \"security\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"item\": \"E03\",\n      \"gate\": \"G2\",\n      \"title\": \"Re-check tools on load\",\n      \"requirement\": \"Take a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.\",\n      \"not_covered\": [\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved\",\n          \"owner\": \"E04\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions\",\n          \"owner\": \"E02\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one\",\n          \"owner\": \"E05\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts\",\n          \"owner\": \"E06\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"rating\": \"gap\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"file\": \"src/integrations/ehr-mcp.ts\",\n          \"line\": 78,\n          \"note\": \"Tools are listed from the EHR server and passed to the registry. No hash of an approved tool's description and schema is stored anywhere (searched src/ and release/).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"E03.1\",\n          \"check\": \"A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema.\",\n          \"met\": \"no\",\n          \"evidence\": \"src/integrations/ehr-mcp.ts:78\",\n          \"quote\": \"c.listTools(),\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"\",\n          \"note\": \"Tools are listed from the EHR server and passed to the registry. No hash of an approved tool's description and schema is stored anywhere (searched src/ and release/).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"E03.2\",\n          \"check\": \"The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition.\",\n          \"met\": \"no\",\n          \"evidence\": \"src/tools/registry.ts:107\",\n          \"quote\": \"const cfg = this.servers.find((s) => s.id === serverId);\",\n          \"counter_search\": \"\",\n          \"note\": \"registerMcp checks only the server id, then the metadata scan. There is no fingerprint compare on load, refresh or reconnect, and no list_changed handler exists (searched src/).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"E03.3\",\n          \"check\": \"When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.\",\n          \"verify\": \"1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cac